//@version=6
// MAR1 Gates - four gate settings, two MA lengths each, all running at once.
//
// maRejection.pine trades ONE configuration: one Percentage, one Min entries to
// start, one MA Length. maSweepPairs.pine then measured the watchlist and said
// which MA Length each gate setting actually wants, and it did not give one
// answer - it gave a grid, one row per gate and one column per timeframe. The
// actual numbers live in the four gate input blocks below, not here - the gates
// are due another round of re-measuring, and a table in a comment would only be
// this file's stalest copy of them.
//
// EVERY CELL EITHER GATE CARRIES WAS MEASURED TOGETHER, pair by pair, timeframe
// by timeframe - the rule this file has held all along. A length that was never
// re-measured stays paired with its partner from whichever sweep last touched
// it.
//
// This file runs all eight of the row that matches the chart's timeframe, side by
// side on one chart, and marks every signal each of them takes. Nothing here is
// a new mechanism: it is maRejection.pine's signal, gate, ladder and window,
// eight independent copies of it, differing in exactly three numbers each.
//
// FOUR GATES, EIGHT MACHINES. A genuine fifth gate would need a fifth pair of
// shapes and there are only two plotshape styles left unused, both of which
// point - see THE EIGHT MARKS at the foot for the arithmetic.
//
// WHICH EIGHT ARE LIVE
// --------------------
// The chart's timeframe picks the column, and only that column. The two
// timeframes are inputs at the top of the dialog - 1m and 3m by default - and
// the chart matching the first runs the first eight lengths, matching the
// second runs the other eight. On any other timeframe NOTHING is drawn and the
// table says why in amber - because the lengths in the grid were measured per
// timeframe, and a length chosen on M1 says nothing about what M5 wants.
// Silence is the honest output there, not a marker.
//
// THE SHAPES - what a marker on the chart tells you
// -------------------------------------------------
// Four things at once, and none of them costs a legend:
//
//   SHAPE       which MACHINE   ▲▼ ⬆⬇ gate A  ● ■ gate B  ⚑ ◆ gate C  ☀ ❄ gate D
//                               eight shapes, one per machine. The two that
//                               POINT point WITH the trade - tip up under the
//                               candle, tip down over it - and the two strokes
//                               are drawn a size up to match the fills
//   CAPTION     which machine,  the gate and the MA under the mark, in the
//               since when     smallest text there is - <gate> · <length> is
//                               the best length, <gate> · <length> the second -
//                               read live from the inputs, so it cannot go
//                               stale. A third piece rides along whenever the
//                               window is open - <gate> · <length> · від 17:30
//                               - the moment it opened, which can be well
//                               before this mark:
//                               a slot that took the lead from another one
//                               that just closed was running the whole time
//                               it was hidden, and this is where that shows
//   SIZE        what it is      the bigger mark is a decision, the smaller one
//                               its outcome. The same pair maRejection.pine
//                               draws, and the two must never be mistakeable
//                               for each other
//   SIDE        direction       below the bar = long, above = short
//   COLOUR      the ladder      white → green → yellow → gold → amber → orange
//                               → RED, one gradient, hotter with every entry
//
// The colour scale is streakColor, copied from maRejection.pine and required to
// stay identical to it - the same run wears the same colour whichever of the two
// files is on the chart. White is the gate one step from opening, the gradient's
// green end is the first entry it allows, and its red end is a run five entries
// past its anchor.
//
// The shape is the part that is new, and it is the whole reason this file can
// exist: with eight machines on one chart, a mark that only carried a colour
// would say "something fired" and leave you to work out which gate owns it.
// Under each one, in the smallest text the chart has, the gate it belongs to -
// read live from the inputs, so it cannot claim a setting that has been edited.
//
// AND THE LEADING SERIES IS THE ONLY ONE THAT DRAWS AT ALL. DISTANCE and
// STRENGTH used to be two more rows in the table above - the leader stood
// nearest the candle at full colour, the other seven further off and washed to
// 90, findable rather than readable. Both are gone: the other seven no longer
// draw AT ALL, so there is nothing left at a distance and nothing left to wash.
//
// WHOEVER WAS THERE FIRST IS THE ONLY ONE THAT SHOWS, and every mark that lands
// after it - a new shape, a new gate, a warning, anything - draws NOTHING until
// that one finishes. Ranked in two tiers:
//
//   an OPEN WINDOW outranks a pre-armed gate, whatever the clock says. A run
//   that is costing money is not to be hidden on behalf of a gate that is
//   merely worth watching.
//
//   inside a tier the EARLIEST wins - the window that opened first, or the gate
//   that reached the white step first.
//
// It cannot be overtaken: a window opening is stamped once and never moves, so
// anything that starts later stands behind it for as long as both are live,
// whichever of the two goes deeper. A run is followed from its beginning to its
// end or it is not a run.
//
// BEST OR SECOND MAKES NO DIFFERENCE to this. Whichever slot started first is
// the one that draws, second length or not.
//
// THE PRICE-DISTANCE MACHINERY IS STILL IN THE FILE - lane(), and the per-slot
// gap in markY and smY - because it used to be the answer to a real problem:
// plot order is fixed when the file compiles while the leading series changes
// every few minutes, so z-order could never be made to follow importance, and a
// white warning on gate D used to sit over a lime entry on gate A purely because
// D is written lower down. With only the leader ever drawing, that machinery now
// only ever has one shape to place on any given bar and always parks it at the
// same distance nearest the candle - kept rather than stripped out, on the
// chance a future change lets more than one machine draw again.
//
// THE OTHER SEVEN ARE HIDDEN NOW, not faded, and that is a deliberate reversal
// of what this file drew before: a signal on a gate that is not carrying the
// episode used to leave a pale mark, and now it leaves none. Nothing about the
// account changes - the gate still grades its own streak, still reaches its own
// row in the RUNNING and CLOSED tables the moment it becomes the leader, and
// still opens its own window if its turn comes. What is gone is chart-only
// evidence of a signal that fired on a gate the chart was not, at that moment,
// ABOUT - which is exactly the clutter this was asked to remove. The RUNNING
// table names the leader with ◀.
//
// THE LIVE PREVIEW
// ----------------
// maRejection.pine's live intrabar preview - the faded marker on the candle
// still forming - IS reproduced now, and it was deliberately left out for a
// stretch of this file's life on the grounds that eight previews would be
// eight markers moving on every tick. That objection stopped applying the
// moment only the leader ever drew: there is at most one settled marker per
// bar now, and the preview is the same one, one candle earlier and faded. See
// LIVE PREVIEW at the foot of the file for the mechanism and why it lives
// there rather than beside the signal it projects.
//
// Settled markers still appear only when the candle closes, on the same
// offset of -1 that MAR1 uses, so they land on the rejection candle. The
// preview is the one exception: it has no offset, because it sits on the
// candle it describes rather than the one after it.
//
// THE TWO TABLES
// --------------
// Both are mar1Scanner.pine's, with their layouts intact - same columns, same
// summary line, same state words. What changes is what a row is: there it is a
// pair, here it is one of the eight machines on this one symbol.
//
//   RUNNING   what is open RIGHT NOW, and only that. A row per gate that has a
//             window open, is armed, or is one step from arming. The header
//             counts all three, so the table can be read without being read.
//   CLOSED    the finished series, newest first, with what each cost. ONE ROW
//             PER EPISODE - mar1GatesScanner.pine's rule, stated in full at
//             logClose: windows that OVERLAP are one episode, because you cannot
//             be in two positions on one pair, and the one that opened first is
//             kept whole while the rest are not recorded. The header carries the
//             total and the average - the same line the scanner prints and the
//             same one maSweepPairs sweeps.
//
// maRejection.pine's PERFORMANCE and distribution tables are NOT here. They
// describe one configuration in twelve metrics; these two describe eight in the
// two numbers a trade is made on.
//
// The CSV export and the attention popup are not here either.
//
// KEEP THE SHARED SETTINGS IDENTICAL to maRejection.pine. Everything below
// 'Signal' is a mirror of that file and must hold its values, or these eight
// machines are grading a strategy nobody trades. Only the three gate blocks are
// meant to differ, and only in Percentage, Min entries and the MA lengths.
// max_labels_count is for the CAPTIONS under the marks - the gate each one
// belongs to, which has to be a label because plotshape's text is a const string
// and this one is read live from the inputs. 500 is the ceiling TradingView
// allows and it is a ROLLING one: the oldest are dropped, so the captions cover
// the recent history while the SHAPES cover all of it.
indicator('MAR1 Gates', overlay = true, max_bars_back = 200, max_labels_count = 500)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The grid
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// First in the dialog, because it is the only part of this file that is touched
// after the first setup. Everything below it is copied from maRejection.pine
// once and then left alone.
//
// Each gate block is six numbers: the two that define the gate, and the four MA
// lengths the sweep chose for it - two per timeframe. The defaults are the grid
// in the header, straight out of maSweepPairs.pine.
//
// The second length is not a spare. Running it beside the best one is how you
// find out whether the best one was a plateau or a spike: if MA 30 and MA 28
// fire together and close together, the choice is safe, and if they behave
// nothing alike then first place in the sweep was luck and neither number should
// be trusted very far.
// NOTHING BELOW PRINTS ITSELF IN THE STATUS LINE, and that is what
// display = display.none on nearly every input in this file buys.
//
// TradingView prints an indicator's ARGUMENTS after its name in the legend, in
// declaration order. Four gate blocks are twenty-eight inputs on their own, and
// with the signal mirrors, the window and the table settings behind them the
// legend ran the whole width of the chart as a wall of bare numbers - '80 5 13
// 14 8 6 90 5 11 8 5 6 98 6 ...' - with nothing saying which number was
// which. A legend that has to be decoded is worse than no legend.
//
// display.none removes an input from that line and from the Data Window and
// changes NOTHING ELSE: the input is fully live, fully editable, and sits in the
// settings dialog exactly where it did. It is a display flag, not a switch, and
// it does not touch the saved-settings POSITION that everything else in these
// files has to be careful about.
//
// TWO ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT VISIBLE, and they are the two that can silently make
// every other number meaningless:
//
//   Streak gate    with Off there is no gate at all, the four blocks collapse
//                  onto each other, and every marker is an entry. Reading the
//                  tables without knowing this is reading fiction.
//   Fire signals   whether the hours filter is actually biting. A stretch of
//   only inside    candles with no markers means 'nothing triggered' when this
//   those hours    is off and 'nothing was allowed to' when it is on, and those
//                  are not the same fact.
//
// TO BRING ANY OTHER ONE BACK: delete ', display = display.none' from its line.
// To hide one of the two above: add it. There is nothing to keep in sync - the
// flag is per input and reaches no logic.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The timeframes
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Which timeframe each COLUMN of the grid was measured on. First in the dialog,
// because everything below reads through them: every gate block carries two MA
// lengths per column, and the chart's timeframe picks the column by matching
// one of these two. On a chart timeframe that matches neither, nothing is
// drawn - the same behaviour the hard-wired 1m/3m pair always had.
//
// VISIBLE in the status line on purpose, like the streak gate and the hours
// filter: these two decide which set of lengths is live, and reading the chart
// without knowing that is reading fiction.
//
// The MA input NAMES below (maA11, maA31, ...) keep the 1 and 3 digits from
// when the columns were hard-wired to 1m and 3m. They are column positions
// now, not timeframes: the column's timeframe is whatever these two inputs
// hold.
gTF = 'Timeframes'
tfA = input.timeframe('1', 'First / second lengths column', inline = 'tf', group = gTF,
     tooltip = 'The timeframe each column of MA lengths was measured on. The chart timeframe picks the column: on the first of these two the first lengths run, on the second the second, and on anything else nothing is drawn and the tables say so. Defaults are the 1m and 3m the grid was always wired to.')
tfB = input.timeframe('3', '', inline = 'tf', group = gTF)

gA = 'Gate A  ·  ▲ triangle + ⬆ arrow'
gB = 'Gate B  ·  ● circle + ■ square'
gC = 'Gate C  ·  ⚑ flag + ◆ diamond'
gD = 'Gate D  ·  ☀ sun + ❄ snowflake'

onA  = input.bool(true, 'Gate A on', group = gA, display = display.none)
pctA = input.float(55.0, 'Percentage  /  Min entries', minval = 1.0, maxval = 100.0, step = 0.05, inline = 'ga', group = gA,
     tooltip = 'Gate A. The percentage and the minimum entries that define this gate, swept on both timeframes.', display = display.none)
minA = input.int(6, '', minval = 1, inline = 'ga', group = gA, display = display.none)
maA11 = input.int(29, 'MA on 1st timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'a1', group = gA,
     tooltip = 'The two lengths maSweepPairs.pine ranked first and second for gate A on the first timeframe. Live only while the chart is on it.', display = display.none)
maA12 = input.int(24, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'a1', group = gA, display = display.none)
maA31 = input.int(8, 'MA on 2nd timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'a3', group = gA,
     tooltip = 'The same two, measured on the second timeframe. Live only while the chart is on it.', display = display.none)
maA32 = input.int(19, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'a3', group = gA, display = display.none)

onB  = input.bool(true, 'Gate B on', group = gB, display = display.none)
pctB = input.float(80.0, 'Percentage  /  Min entries', minval = 1.0, maxval = 100.0, step = 0.05, inline = 'gb', group = gB,
     tooltip = 'Gate B. The percentage and the minimum entries that define this gate, swept on both timeframes.', display = display.none)
minB = input.int(7, '', minval = 1, inline = 'gb', group = gB, display = display.none)
maB11 = input.int(6, 'MA on 1st timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'b1', group = gB,
     tooltip = 'The two lengths maSweepPairs.pine ranked first and second for gate B on the first timeframe.', display = display.none)
maB12 = input.int(7, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'b1', group = gB, display = display.none)
maB31 = input.int(13, 'MA on 2nd timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'b3', group = gB,
     tooltip = 'The same two on the second timeframe. Live while the chart is on it.', display = display.none)
maB32 = input.int(6, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'b3', group = gB, display = display.none)

onC  = input.bool(true, 'Gate C on', group = gC, display = display.none)
pctC = input.float(90.0, 'Percentage  /  Min entries', minval = 1.0, maxval = 100.0, step = 0.05, inline = 'gc', group = gC,
     tooltip = 'Gate C. The percentage and the minimum entries that define this gate, swept on both timeframes.', display = display.none)
minC = input.int(8, '', minval = 1, inline = 'gc', group = gC, display = display.none)
maC11 = input.int(41, 'MA on 1st timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'c1', group = gC, display = display.none)
maC12 = input.int(24, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'c1', group = gC, display = display.none)
maC31 = input.int(5, 'MA on 2nd timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'c3', group = gC, display = display.none)
maC32 = input.int(6, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'c3', group = gC, display = display.none)

onD  = input.bool(true, 'Gate D on', group = gD, display = display.none)
pctD = input.float(95.0, 'Percentage  /  Min entries', minval = 1.0, maxval = 100.0, step = 0.05, inline = 'gd', group = gD,
     tooltip = 'Gate D. The percentage and the minimum entries that define this gate, swept on both timeframes.', display = display.none)
minD = input.int(9, '', minval = 1, inline = 'gd', group = gD, display = display.none)
maD11 = input.int(9, 'MA on 1st timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'd1', group = gD, display = display.none)
maD12 = input.int(24, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'd1', group = gD, display = display.none)
maD31 = input.int(9, 'MA on 2nd timeframe:  best  /  second', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'd3', group = gD, display = display.none)
maD32 = input.int(8, '', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, inline = 'd3', group = gD, display = display.none)

// Which column of the grid the chart is asking for.
//
// The two timeframes are the tfA / tfB inputs at the top of the dialog - they
// used to be hard-wired to 1m and 3m behind a TF3_ON constant, and the whole
// mechanism became these two inputs (2026-08-13, user request).
//
// The comparison runs through timeframe.in_seconds rather than string
// equality, so '60' and '1H' name the same column instead of accidentally
// missing each other. Both inputs are SIMPLE strings, so the lengths chosen
// from them stay simple ints - which is the only reason they can be handed to
// ta.ema at all. A series length would be refused before the first bar.
//
// On a timeframe that matches neither, the first column's lengths are still
// handed to the moving averages - a length has to be SOMETHING - but tfOk is
// false and nothing reaches the chart. See the note in the header: a length
// measured on one timeframe is not evidence about another, and drawing it
// there would be inventing a result.
//
// The same timeframe in both inputs is not an error: the first column simply
// wins the tie and the second one never runs.

// What the tables call the timeframes they accept. One string, so a message
// cannot claim a timeframe is welcome while the inputs say otherwise.
TF_LIVE = tfA + ' OR ' + tfB

secA  = timeframe.in_seconds(tfA)
secB  = timeframe.in_seconds(tfB)
tfIsB = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period) == secB and secB != secA
tfOk  = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period) == secA or tfIsB

lenA1 = tfIsB ? maA31 : maA11
lenA2 = tfIsB ? maA32 : maA12
lenB1 = tfIsB ? maB31 : maB11
lenB2 = tfIsB ? maB32 : maB12
lenC1 = tfIsB ? maC31 : maC11
lenC2 = tfIsB ? maC32 : maC12
lenD1 = tfIsB ? maD31 : maD11
lenD2 = tfIsB ? maD32 : maD12

// WHETHER A SLOT IS TICKED, and it is declared HERE - above everything that
// reads it - rather than beside the tables, which is where it used to live.
//
// It had to move because the CLOSED LOG was not consulting it. Every other
// output already did: the marks, their captions, the outcome marks, the alerts,
// the leading series and the RUNNING table all die with their tick box. The log
// did not, because it is written from INSIDE the machine - logClose is called at
// the moment a window closes, four hundred lines above the first mention of
// slotOn - so unticking gate D cleared the candles and the live table and left
// gate D's closures sitting in the log, feeding the count and the average on the
// header line. A tick box that silences a chart while a table keeps quoting the
// same machine is worse than no tick box: it makes the two disagree.
//
// The machines still RUN when unticked. A call site cannot be made conditional
// in Pine, and this is the switch on their OUTPUT.
slotOn(int s) => s < 2 ? onA : s < 4 ? onB : s < 6 ? onC : onD

// How many slots are live, for the two footers. It was worked out inside the
// RUNNING table's drawing block, which meant the CLOSED log could not reach it -
// and the log is now the table that most needs to say it, because the average on
// its header is over whatever is ticked.
liveGates = (onA ? 2 : 0) + (onB ? 2 : 0) + (onC ? 2 : 0) + (onD ? 2 : 0)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Signal settings - mirrors of maRejection.pine
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MA Length is absent on purpose: eight of them are in the grid above. Percentage
// and Min entries to start are absent for the same reason. Everything else here
// must hold the values maRejection.pine holds.
gSig  = 'Signal (must match MAR1)'
gFilt = 'Filters (must match MAR1)'
gWin  = 'Closed log window'
gOut  = 'Table'

maType   = input.string('EMA', 'MA Type', options = ['SMA', 'EMA', 'WMA', 'RMA'], group = gSig, display = display.none)
src      = input.source(ohlc4, 'Source', group = gSig, display = display.none)

trendBars = input.int(3, 'Bars that must close on the trend side of MA', minval = 2, maxval = 10, group = gSig, display = display.none)
trendSep  = input.float(0.30, 'Min distance of trend closes from MA (x ATR)', minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
wickRatio = input.float(0.60, 'Min wick share of candle range', minval = 0.30, maxval = 0.95, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
touchTol  = input.float(0.30, 'Almost-reached tolerance (x ATR)', minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
atrLen    = input.int(3, 'ATR length', minval = 2, group = gSig, display = display.none)

allowBodyInside    = input.bool(true, 'Accept small body fully on the trend side', group = gSig, display = display.none)
allowLongWick      = input.bool(true, 'Accept wick longer than its running average', group = gSig, display = display.none)
allowCounterCandle = input.bool(true, 'Accept a single counter-trend candle', group = gSig, display = display.none)

// The two-candle module. Constants rather than inputs, for the reason spelled
// out in maRejection.pine: an input declared mid-file shifts every setting under
// it on every chart already carrying the script. These three must match the
// block of the same name in the other files - nothing checks them for you.
DC_ON   = true
DC_BACK = 4
DC_REF  = 'Close'

refLen = input.int(200, 'Average window (candles)', minval = 10, maxval = 200, group = gSig, display = display.none)

minPips              = input.float(1.0, 'Min wick length (pips)', minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = gFilt, display = display.none)
cooldown             = input.int(1, 'Min bars between signals', minval = 1, group = gFilt, display = display.none)
perDirectionCooldown = input.bool(true, 'Cooldown per direction', group = gFilt, display = display.none)
gateMode             = input.string('Max of both', 'Streak gate', options = ['Off', 'Manual', 'Distribution', 'Max of both'], group = gFilt,
     tooltip = 'Shared by all four gates. It decides HOW the threshold is built; Percentage and Min entries in each gate block decide what it is built from. With Off there is no gate at all and the four blocks collapse onto each other - eight machines then differ only by MA length.')

startHour       = input.int(4, "Start indicator's hours", group = gFilt, display = display.none)
endHour         = input.int(16, "End indicator's hours", group = gFilt, display = display.none)
restrictToHours = input.bool(true, 'Fire signals only inside those hours', group = gFilt)

// Which successes close a window. Only the window counter reads it - the entries
// in the table's Window column are counted against this definition - and it must
// match the other files or that column describes something else.
successScope = input.string('Entries only', 'A window is closed by',
     options = ['Entries only', 'Entries and warnings', 'All found signals'], group = gFilt, display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Which stretch of time the closed log covers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Declared HERE, four hundred lines above the table it controls, and that is not
// where it belongs on a dialog - it is where it has to be in a file. The window
// decides what gets RECORDED, and the recording happens inside the machine; an
// input declared under the table would not exist yet when the first closure is
// written. So the window travels with the data and the position settings travel
// with the table.
//
// Two questions, kept apart, exactly as mar1Scanner.pine splits them:
//
//   HOW LONG    a number of hours, or a calendar unit, or no limit at all
//   WHERE       rolling with the clock, pinned to an hour of today, or pinned
//               to a named day
//
// They used to be one dropdown with a Custom entry, which meant setting a mode
// and then two numbers that were ignored in every other mode - nothing appeared
// to happen until all three agreed. Split, the length is always live and the
// anchor is the only thing deciding whether the stretch follows the clock.
//
// Every combination means something and it means the obvious thing: the date
// says WHERE the window starts, the length says HOW LONG it runs. A Monday
// chosen with Day gives that Monday, with Week gives that week, with Everything
// gives that Monday onwards.
//
// Everything is the default here, unlike in the scanner. This file logs ONE
// symbol, so a day of it is a short list - and the first question asked of eight
// machines on one chart is how they compare over as much history as loaded.
jrnLength = input.string('Everything', 'Window length',
     options = ['1 hour', '2 hours', '4 hours', '8 hours', '12 hours', '16 hours', '24 hours', 'Custom', 'Day', 'Week', 'Everything'], group = gWin,
     tooltip = 'Day and Week ignore the anchor below - they start at midnight UTC+1 and at Monday. 24 hours is not the same as Day: it rolls back a full day from now, across midnight. Custom reads its length from the hours field below.', display = display.none)

jrnAnchor = input.string('Ending now', 'Window anchored', options = ['Ending now', 'Starting at hour', 'Starting at date'], group = gWin,
     tooltip = 'Ending now rolls with the clock. Starting at hour pins a stretch of TODAY. Starting at date pins a stretch of a named day, which is the only one that can read back a day that has already ended.', display = display.none)

jrnFromHour = input.int(9, 'Starting hour, UTC+1', minval = 0, maxval = 23, group = gWin,
     tooltip = 'Only used when the anchor above is Starting at hour and the length is in hours.', display = display.none)

// A float because a typed length is not a whole number of hours: 2.5 and 0.75
// are ordinary things to ask for, and rounding them to the hour would answer a
// question nobody asked.
jrnHours = input.float(3.0, 'Custom window length, hours', minval = 0.05, maxval = 168.0, step = 0.25, group = gWin,
     tooltip = "Used only when 'Window length' is Custom. Fractions are honoured - 2.5 is two and a half hours, 0.75 is forty-five minutes.", display = display.none)

// 1767225600000 is 2026-01-01 00:00 UTC in milliseconds, and it is a literal
// rather than a timestamp() call because input.time demands a CONST int and
// timestamp() does not give one - its numeric form reads the exchange timezone,
// which cannot be known at compile time. It is only where the picker opens.
jrnFrom = input.time(1767225600000, 'Custom window starts', group = gWin,
     tooltip = "Used only when 'Window anchored' is Starting at date. The window runs from here for the length above. The log's header prints the window that was actually applied, in UTC+1 - check it there rather than assuming.", display = display.none)

// The Today button the date picker does not have. A button would set the date
// once and be forgotten, so tomorrow the log would still be pinned to today's
// date; a tick box follows the calendar instead. The picked date is left intact
// underneath and comes back when this is unticked.
jrnToday = input.bool(false, 'Custom window starts today (ignores the date)', group = gWin,
     tooltip = "Overrides the date above with today's midnight, and follows the calendar - it still means today tomorrow.", display = display.none)

// Repeated as a constant so the header can tell a date that was CHOSEN from one
// that was never touched. Without it the only honest warning would be permanent,
// and a warning that is always there is one nobody reads.
JRN_FROM_DEFAULT = 1767225600000

// UTC+1 for the calendar boundaries, the frame the hours filter and every
// timestamp in this project already use. Epoch day 0 was a Thursday, hence the 3
// in the week arithmetic: it puts the boundary on Monday.
//
// Built from timenow, the WALL CLOCK, and not from the bar's own time. That is
// the whole trick: timenow reads the same on a bar from last Tuesday as on the
// live one, so "is this closure inside the window" has an answer at every point
// in history - and the window is complete the moment the script loads rather
// than filling up only from when the tab was opened.
utcShift = 3600000
dayMs    = 86400000
hourMs   = 3600000

nowDayIdx  = math.floor((timenow + utcShift) / dayMs)
nowWeekIdx = math.floor((nowDayIdx + 3) / 7)

todayStart = nowDayIdx * dayMs - utcShift
weekStart  = (7 * nowWeekIdx - 3) * dayMs - utcShift

// Zero means the length is a calendar unit rather than a count of hours, which
// is also what makes the anchor inapplicable - Day and Week already know where
// they start.
lengthHours = jrnLength == '1 hour' ? 1.0 :
     jrnLength == '2 hours' ? 2.0 :
     jrnLength == '4 hours' ? 4.0 :
     jrnLength == '8 hours' ? 8.0 :
     jrnLength == '12 hours' ? 12.0 :
     jrnLength == '16 hours' ? 16.0 :
     jrnLength == '24 hours' ? 24.0 :
     jrnLength == 'Custom' ? jrnHours :
     0.0

lengthMs = int(lengthHours * hourMs)

calMs = jrnLength == 'Day' ? dayMs :
     jrnLength == 'Week' ? 7 * dayMs :
     0

// What was TYPED versus what the window ends up being. Zero span is unbounded,
// which only Everything produces.
spanMs = lengthHours > 0 ? lengthMs : calMs

datePinned = jrnAnchor == 'Starting at date'
hourPinned = lengthHours > 0 and jrnAnchor == 'Starting at hour'
pinned     = datePinned or hourPinned

// The Today tick, resolved once and in one place, so the picked date and its
// override cannot disagree between the window and the header that prints it.
customStart = jrnToday ? todayStart : jrnFrom

pinFrom = datePinned ? customStart : todayStart + jrnFromHour * hourMs

// The date anchor is read FIRST, so a calendar length cannot silently overrule
// it by picking its own start behind it.
periodFrom = datePinned ? customStart :
     jrnLength == 'Day' ? todayStart :
     jrnLength == 'Week' ? weekStart :
     jrnLength == 'Everything' ? 0 :
     hourPinned ? pinFrom :
     timenow - lengthMs

// A day past the wall clock is past everything that has closed, since a closure
// is a candle that has already ended. Only a BOUNDED pinned window has a real
// ceiling - without one, naming 09:00 for an hour would show the morning and
// everything after it.
periodTo = pinned and spanMs > 0 ? pinFrom + spanMs : timenow + dayMs

txtSizeName = input.string('Small', 'Text size', options = ['Tiny', 'Small', 'Normal'], group = gOut, display = display.none)
showTable   = input.bool(false, 'Show the live gate table', group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'Off by default. Eight gates put thirty-two markers on the chart already, and this table is for reading back rather than for standing between the candles and the eye.', display = display.none)

// Applies to every cell of both tables at once. Resolved here, beside the input
// it reads, rather than down in the drawing block - it was in the drawing block
// once and disappeared with it when the tables were replaced, which cost thirty
// compile errors and is exactly the kind of coupling a setting should not have
// to the code that happens to use it.
txtSize = txtSizeName == 'Tiny' ? size.tiny : txtSizeName == 'Normal' ? size.normal : size.small

// One tier up, for the Gate column alone. That cell is the one that says WHICH
// of the eight a row belongs to, and at the size of its neighbours it was the
// easiest thing on the row to miss. A table cell CAN take its size from a
// setting, unlike a plotshape, whose size argument is const - so this follows
// the text-size preference instead of being fixed the way the markers are.
txtSizeBig = txtSizeName == 'Tiny' ? size.small : txtSizeName == 'Normal' ? size.large : size.normal

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The colour ladder
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Copied verbatim from streakColor in maRejection.pine, and it has to stay that
// way: a run four entries past its anchor is red on both files or the two are
// describing different things with the same picture. mar1Scanner.pine carries
// the same scale as a step number, because a colour cannot cross
// request.security - see ladderStep and stepColor there.
//
//   limit - 1   white       one short, the gate is about to open
//   limit       #22C55E     entry 1 - bright green, a normal cost
//   +1          #15803D     entry 2 - a visibly deeper green
//   +2          #166534     entry 3 - the deepest green, still clearly green
//   +3          #EA580C     entry 4 - the jump: green ends here, hot starts
//   +4          #DC2626     entry 5 - a visibly hotter red
//   +5 beyond   #450A0A     entry 6 on - near-black red, a run to avoid
//   below       faded       carries nothing from this scale
//
// ONE SUCCESSIVE WARM GRADIENT (2026-08-10, user request), through six passes
// the same day - neon accent shades, then Material's 700-900 shades, then
// Tailwind's 700/900 shades for staying saturated rather than greying out as
// they darken, then moving the boundary itself so entries 1-3 are all shades
// of green with an abrupt jump to entry 4's orange, then widening the SPREAD
// inside each half so the three greens and the three reds are each a real
// jump apart, and finally pulling entry 3 back from #14532D to #166534 -
// that dark washed out at the transparency the boxes and rows actually draw
// at - while entry 2 moved from #16A34A to #15803D so 1 vs 2 is a real jump
// too - see streakColor in maRejection.pine for the full reasoning. White at
// the bottom is unchanged and deliberately outside the gradient: it is
// pre-armed, not a cost step.
streakColor(int streak, int limit) =>
    if streak == limit - 1
        color.white
    else if streak == limit
        #22C55E
    else if streak > limit
        if streak - limit < 2
            #15803D
        else if streak - limit < 3
            #166534
        else if streak - limit < 4
            #EA580C
        else if streak - limit < 5
            #DC2626
        else
            // The one colour on the whole ladder that should read as "stop" -
            // see streakColor in maRejection.pine for the full reasoning.
            #450A0A
    else
        color.new(color.silver, 80)

// The rung a window of n entries closed on. The ladder runs one step BEHIND the
// entry count - a window that took four entries reached +3 and is #EA580C, one
// that took five reached +4 and is #DC2626 - so this is n + 1, held at the top.
//
// A number rather than a colour, because the closed log stores it and an array
// of colours would be an array of things that cannot be compared or summed.
// EIGHT is the step past the gradient's own end, and it is 8 rather than 7
// because 7 is taken: mar1Scanner.pine and mar1GatesScanner.pine both use it
// for "below the gate, faded", a reading this file has no number for but
// shares a scale with. Numbering the new step 7 here would have made the same
// integer mean the bottom of the ladder in one file and the top of it in
// another.
ladderRung(int n) =>
    n <= 0 ? 0 : n >= 6 ? 8 : n + 1

// The ladder again, as a FILL rather than as text - what both tables paint
// its rows with. Copied from stepColor in mar1Scanner.pine, transparency
// argument included. The top step gets 25 less than it is asked for: the
// darkest step of the gradient is the one hue that has to stay loud when
// everything around it is a wash.
rungFill(int step, int transp) =>
    step == 1 ? color.new(color.white, transp) :
     step == 2 ? color.new(#22C55E, transp) :
     step == 3 ? color.new(#15803D, transp) :
     step == 4 ? color.new(#166534, transp) :
     step == 5 ? color.new(#EA580C, transp) :
     step == 6 ? color.new(#DC2626, transp > 25 ? transp - 25 : 0) :
     // The step ABOVE the gradient's own end, so it gets the same loud
     // treatment: less transparency than the caller asked for. A run six
     // entries deep is the last thing on either table that should blend in.
     step == 8 ? color.new(#450A0A, transp > 25 ? transp - 25 : 0) :
     color.new(#333333, 60)

// Only white needs black text. The 700-900 shades the gradient runs on are all
// dark enough on their own to take white, deep tones being the point of them.
rungText(int step) =>
    step == 1 ? color.black : color.white

// Index at which the cumulative distribution of the streak histogram crosses
// perc. Identical to calculateIndex in maRejection.pine, including the inclusive
// comparison - a strict one slides the answer one bucket to the right.
calcIndex(int[] arr, float perc) =>
    total = 0.0
    for i = 1 to array.size(arr) - 1
        total += array.get(arr, i)
    if total == 0
        -1
    else
        cumSum = 0.0
        index = -1
        for i = 1 to array.size(arr) - 1
            cumSum += array.get(arr, i) / total * 100
            if cumSum >= perc and index == -1
                index := i
                break
        index

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Everything a moving average does not enter into
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Computed once and shared by all eight machines. This is the same split
// maSweepPairs.pine makes and for the same reason: the ATR, the wick averages,
// the body average, the geometry and both counter-trend paths do not depend on
// the MA, so computing them eight times would be eight times the work for one
// answer.
isBull = open < close
isBear = open > close

bodySize  = math.abs(close - open)
atrVal    = ta.atr(atrLen)
bodyRef   = ta.sma(bodySize, refLen)[1]
rangeSize = high - low
upWick    = high - math.max(open, close)
dnWick    = math.min(open, close) - low
upWickRef = ta.sma(upWick, refLen)[1]
dnWickRef = ta.sma(dnWick, refLen)[1]

pipSize = syminfo.mintick * (syminfo.type == 'forex' ? 10.0 : 1.0)
tolVal  = atrVal * touchTol

// Length-independent facts every machine reads, hoisted out of gateOne so they
// are computed once per bar rather than once per machine. Same values, same
// operand order - only the repetition is gone.
sepNow        = atrVal * trendSep
bodyRefReady  = not na(bodyRef)
gateIsOff     = gateMode == 'Off'
gateIsManual  = gateMode == 'Manual'
gateIsDist    = gateMode == 'Distribution'
gateNeedsDist = gateIsDist or gateMode == 'Max of both'
scopeAllFound = successScope == 'All found signals'
scopeWarns    = successScope == 'Entries and warnings'

wickRejectDown = upWick >= wickRatio * rangeSize and upWick >= minPips * pipSize
wickRejectUp   = dnWick >= wickRatio * rangeSize and dnWick >= minPips * pipSize
smallBody      = bodyRefReady and bodySize < bodyRef
longWickDown   = allowLongWick and not na(upWickRef) and upWick > upWickRef
longWickUp     = allowLongWick and not na(dnWickRef) and dnWick > dnWickRef

counterShort = allowCounterCandle and isBull and isBear[1]
counterLong  = allowCounterCandle and isBear and isBull[1]

dcRef   = DC_REF == 'Open' ? open[DC_BACK] : close[DC_BACK]
dcReady = DC_ON and not na(dcRef)
dcShort = dcReady and isBull and isBull[1] and isBear[2] and close <= dcRef
dcLong  = dcReady and isBear and isBear[1] and isBull[2] and close >= dcRef

// UTC+1, the frame the hours filter and every timestamp in this project use.
localHour = (hour + 1) % 24
inHours   = localHour >= startHour and localHour < endHour
hoursOk   = not restrictToHours or inHours

// Whether the session's hour window just closed on THIS bar - restrictToHours
// on, inHours true a bar ago and false now. This is the boundary a window
// still RUNNING at that moment gets logged against (see LOG_ENTRY_TIMES and
// gateOne's own use of this flag) - a bookkeeping fact for the Pine Logs
// export only. Nothing here touches windowOpen, entriesInWindow or the streak:
// the machine itself does not know the session ended and keeps running into
// tomorrow exactly as it always has, restrictToHours already suppressing new
// signals in the meantime the same way it always did.
//
// Declared here, right beside inHours, rather than reusing hoursCloses further
// down beside the hours shading - that one computes the identical fact for a
// different reader, and duplicating the expression here rather than reaching
// for it is what keeps the shading and the log from being coupled through one
// shared name neither of them is really about.
//
// restrictToHours is part of the test on purpose - 'якщо сесія виконання
// вказана' (user, 2026-08-11): with the hours filter off there is no session
// boundary to log against at all, and inHours would otherwise still flip once
// a day on its own by construction.
sessionJustEnded = restrictToHours and not inHours and inHours[1]

// --- MODULE: flat filter ----------------------------------------------------
// A stretch where price walks a long way and arrives nowhere, and where this
// strategy spends entries for nothing: the trend test still passes on the
// micro-swings inside it, both directions fire within minutes of each other,
// and almost all of them fail.
//
// MEASURED AS AN EFFICIENCY RATIO - net displacement divided by the path
// actually walked, over flatLen bars. 1.0 is a straight line, 0.0 is a walk
// that ended where it started.
//
// Chosen over every width test - a narrow box, a Bollinger squeeze, a low ATR -
// because the flats that cost the most are NOT quiet. The candles are full
// size, they simply cancel each other out, and a width test looks at that and
// sees an ordinary market.
//
// PRICE ONLY, no moving average anywhere in it, and that is structural rather
// than stylistic: in the scanner files the same block has to be computed ONCE
// PER PAIR in the shared block. Read it off an MA instead and thirty pairs
// carrying eight lengths each would produce two hundred and forty different
// verdicts about thirty stretches of price.
//
// TWO THRESHOLDS, NOT ONE. It enters flat below flatLo and leaves only above
// flatHi, which is higher. With a single threshold the verdict flickers bar
// by bar around it and windows end up half blocked, which is worse than no
// filter at all.
//
// AND IT KEEPS STANDING ASIDE FOR flatWait BARS AFTER THE FLAT ENDS. The
// candle that breaks a range is exactly the wick-rejection candle this strategy
// likes, and it is also the one most often taken straight back.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT DO is close anything. It is wired into buyFound / sellFound,
// the same place the hours filter is wired into, so an open window keeps its
// entry count and its rung and simply stands still until the flat ends - the
// ladder is DEFERRED, never abandoned. That also means a winning signal cannot
// land during a flat, so a window cannot close inside one either. The machine
// is paused whole, exactly as it already is overnight.
//
// AND THE STREAK DOES NOT MOVE while it is paused, for free, because the
// grading below reads buyFound / sellFound: a suppressed signal never reaches
// the histogram, so a flat cannot inflate the gate threshold or the failure
// distribution. Same behaviour the hours filter has always had.
//
// INPUTS RATHER THAN CONSTANTS, and where they sit is chosen rather than
// convenient. They have to be declared ABOVE the machine that reads them, which
// is the middle of the file, and TradingView maps saved settings onto inputs BY
// POSITION - so every input declared BELOW this line moves down five places on
// any chart already carrying the script. That is a one-off Defaults -> Reset
// settings, and it is what tuning the filter from the dialog costs.
//
// They shipped as constants for exactly one revision, for the reason the
// two-candle module above still is one: to cost nobody a reset. That was the
// wrong trade here. Thresholds that can only be found by trying them are not
// something to edit in six source files at a time.
//
// Unticking 'Stand aside while flat' restores the behaviour this file had before
// the module existed, the shading included.
//
// These five must hold the same values in maRejection.pine, mar1Gates.pine,
// mar1GatesScanner.pine, mar1Scanner.pine, maSweep.pine and maSweepPairs.pine.
// Nothing checks that for you - they are separate inputs on separate scripts,
// and two of them disagreeing is a strategy measured under one filter and traded
// under another.
gFlat = 'Flat filter'

// Left VISIBLE in the status line, like the streak gate and the hours filter and
// for the same reason: with it on, a stretch of candles carrying no markers
// means 'nothing was allowed to fire' rather than 'nothing triggered', and
// reading the chart without knowing which is reading fiction.
flatOn = input.bool(true, 'Stand aside while flat', group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'Suppresses the DETECTED signal, in exactly the place the hours filter does. An open window keeps its entries and its rung and stands still until the flat ends - the ladder is deferred, never abandoned - and the streak does not move while it is paused. Untick to get the behaviour this file had before the filter existed.')
flatLen = input.int(4, 'Bars measured', minval = 2, maxval = 500, group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'How far back the efficiency ratio looks: net displacement over these bars divided by the path actually walked. Shorter reacts sooner and calls more stretches flat; longer only notices the long ones.', display = display.none)
flatLo = input.float(0.2, 'Enter flat below  /  leave above', minval = 0.01, maxval = 1.0, step = 0.01, inline = 'fl', group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'Two thresholds, and the second must be the higher of the two. The state enters flat below the first and leaves only above the second - with a single threshold the verdict flickers bar by bar around it and windows end up half blocked, which is worse than no filter at all. On the ratio itself, 1.0 is a straight line and 0.0 is a walk that ended where it started.', display = display.none)
flatHi = input.float(0.45, '', minval = 0.01, maxval = 1.0, step = 0.01, inline = 'fl', group = gFlat, display = display.none)
flatWait = input.int(0, 'Bars to keep standing aside after it ends', minval = 0, maxval = 100, group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'The candle that breaks a range is exactly the wick-rejection candle this strategy likes, and it is also the one most often taken straight back. 0 resumes on the breakout candle itself.', display = display.none)

// na for the first flatLen bars, because math.sum propagates na - and na fails
// BOTH comparisons below, so the state simply stays where it is during warmup
// rather than reading a zero ratio as a flat.
flatPath = math.sum(math.abs(close - close[1]), flatLen)
flatEr   = nz(flatPath) > 0 ? math.abs(close - close[flatLen]) / flatPath : float(na)

var bool flatNow  = false
var int  flatLeft = -100000

// Confirmed bars only. The verdict has to hold still for the whole of a forming
// candle, or the signal read from that candle changes under the reader.
enteredThisBar = false
if barstate.isconfirmed
    if flatNow
        if flatEr > flatHi
            flatNow  := false
            flatLeft := bar_index
    else if flatEr < flatLo
        flatNow := true
        enteredThisBar := true

// The bar flat turns on does not stand aside for its OWN close - only
// from the next bar. A signal graded this bar reads triggerLong[1], a
// pattern that completed on the PREVIOUS bar, and the live preview held the
// not-yet-flat verdict stable through this whole bar's formation (flatNow
// never changes mid-candle, only right here at confirmation) - so grading
// this same bar against the flat state its own close just produced
// contradicted what the preview had been showing the whole time it formed
// (user report, 2026-08-10: a losing preview mark vanished on confirmation
// because the close that graded it also flipped flatNow in the same
// instant). Leaving flat is unaffected - flatWait already owns how soon
// trading resumes after that, and 0 has always meant the breakout candle
// itself; only entering is deferred.
flatHold = (flatNow and not enteredThisBar) or bar_index - flatLeft < flatWait
flatOk   = not (flatOn and flatHold)

// LIVE, unlike everything above - this second copy is NOT gated by
// barstate.isconfirmed, so it tracks flatEr on every realtime tick instead of
// freezing until the bar closes. It exists for exactly one reader, livePrev()
// near the foot of the file, and it is the fix for a gap the confirmed-only
// version above cannot close on its own.
//
// The star preview on a forming candle used to be gated on flatOk - the
// CONFIRMED, frozen verdict, unavoidably one candle stale during formation
// (see the note above flatHold: that freeze is deliberate, and correct for
// what it is protecting). A pattern previewed on candle N is not graded until
// candle N+1's close, reading triggerLong[1] one bar back - so the star shown
// throughout candle N's formation was being judged against flatOk as it stood
// BEFORE candle N even confirmed, while the entry it previews will actually
// be graded against flatOk as it stands AFTER candle N+1 confirms. Two
// confirmations, one number, and the number is free to change between them.
// Concretely: flatEr genuinely crosses into a flat while candle N is still
// forming, the star holds steady through the close because the confirmed
// flatOk has not moved yet, and only surfaces as a flat a candle later - user
// report, a star that looked fine through its own close and then turned out
// to belong to a flat once its entry would have graded.
//
// This cannot be made exact - the settled grading a bar later still depends
// on where flatEr goes between now and then, which cannot be known yet. It is
// the same kind of accepted imprecision as the unprojected gateThreshold in
// livePrev(): a smaller, honest gap in exchange for a preview that follows
// the market instead of an already-stale reading of it.
//
// No enteredThisBar exception here, unlike flatHold above - that exception
// exists purely to keep a FROZEN preview consistent with a SETTLED grade one
// step behind it. A live reading has nothing behind it to stay consistent
// with; it only has to say what the ratio is doing right now.
var bool flatNowLive  = false
var int  flatLeftLive = -100000

if flatNowLive
    if flatEr > flatHi
        flatNowLive  := false
        flatLeftLive := bar_index
else if flatEr < flatLo
    flatNowLive := true

flatHoldLive = flatNowLive or bar_index - flatLeftLive < flatWait
flatOkLive   = not (flatOn and flatHoldLive)
// --- MODULE: flat filter ------------------------------------------------ END

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The closed log
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Seven parallel arrays rather than one array of records, for the same reason
// mar1Scanner.pine uses them: it is the shape the table renderer wants, and Pine
// has no tuple array.
//
// The machine pushes into these directly. A Pine function cannot assign to a
// global, but an array is a reference - so a closure can be recorded from inside
// the call site that produced it, which is the only place that knows about it.
//
// Newest LAST in the arrays and newest FIRST on screen: pushing is O(1) and the
// renderer walks backwards. Doing it the other way round would mean shifting
// every row on every closure.
var int[] jGate  = array.new_int(0)     // which slot, 0..7
var int[] jOpen  = array.new_int(0)     // when the window opened, ms
var int[] jClose = array.new_int(0)     // when it closed
var int[] jEnt   = array.new_int(0)     // what it cost
var int[] jDir   = array.new_int(0)     // 1 long, -1 short
var int[] jStep  = array.new_int(0)     // the rung it closed on, for the colour

// The key the dedup RANKS by, which is not the key it DISPLAYS. jOpen is the
// first entry, because that is what a closed series is dated from in every file
// in this project. This is when the window OPENED - the green diamond - because
// that is what the chart leads with, and the row kept here has to be the machine
// the chart actually drew or the two disagree about the same morning.
var int[] jRank  = array.new_int(0)

// The ceiling that applies to the log, and it exists for one reason: Pine
// refuses an array past 100,000 elements and there are seven of them. It is
// reported on the header line rather than quietly dropping rows.
JRN_MAX = 20000

// A constant rather than an input, for the reason DC_ON above it already is:
// adding an input here would shift every input declared after it - and this
// file has dozens after this point - which costs a Defaults -> Reset on every
// chart already carrying the script. To turn this off, flip it to false and
// re-add the file to the chart, exactly like DC_ON.
//
// Gates every Pine Logs line this file writes (user request, 2026-08-11):
// the per-entry timestamps inside a closed episode, a periodic line for
// whatever is still RUNNING and not yet closed, and the session-boundary
// line below for a window still open when the hours filter's session ends.
// None of it touches the chart, the tables or the trading logic - it is a
// second, text-only export next to the closed log's own arrays, read back
// from the Pine Editor's Pine Logs tab rather than from the chart.
LOG_ENTRY_TIMES = true

// A slot-only label for the Pine Logs lines below - not slotGlyph/slotTag,
// which live much later in the file and also need the MA length. This one
// only needs the slot number, so it can be declared here, beside logClose(),
// well before slotGlyph exists.
slotName(int s) => s == 0 ? 'A1' : s == 1 ? 'A2' : s == 2 ? 'B1' : s == 3 ? 'B2' :
     s == 4 ? 'C1' : s == 5 ? 'C2' : s == 6 ? 'D1' : 'D2'

// entryTimes, comma separated, same 'MM-dd HH:mm' UTC+1 format the Opened /
// Closed columns already use - so a line pasted out of Pine Logs reads in the
// same units as the table beside it. Used by gateOne() at three points: a
// closed episode's full breakdown, a periodic line for whatever is still
// RUNNING, and the session-boundary line below - one function so the three
// can never format the same fact three different ways.
joinTimes(int[] arr) =>
    string s = ''
    if array.size(arr) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(arr) - 1
            s := s + (i > 0 ? ',' : '') + str.format_time(array.get(arr, i), 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1')
    s

// A closure outside the window is not recorded at all, rather than recorded and
// cleaned up afterwards. On the historical pass that is most of them, and the
// arrays never hold what they are about to throw away.
//
// The UPPER bound is what makes the setting a window rather than a cutoff:
// without it, naming 09:00 for one hour would show the morning and everything
// after it.
//
// time_close, not time: the row is the moment the window FINISHED, which is the
// end of the winning candle. It is also what the ageing loop compares against,
// and what mar1Scanner.pine stamps its own log with - the two files date the
// same event the same way or their logs cannot be read side by side.
// ONE ROW PER EPISODE, which is a WIDER rule than one row per closing minute,
// and the reason it had to widen is the account rather than the arithmetic.
//
//   YOU CANNOT BE IN TWO POSITIONS ON ONE PAIR. Once money is in on one gate,
//   a second gate opening on the same symbol is not a trade you would take - it
//   is the same money, seen by another machine. So a series that started while
//   another was still running never happened as far as a ledger is concerned,
//   and a row for it is a row for a trade nobody made.
//
// The closing-minute rule only caught the case where two gates finished on the
// SAME candle. It let this through:
//
//   ◆ 27   12:00 -> 12:30   6 entries
//   ◇ 30   12:05 -> 12:33   6 entries
//
// Two rows, twelve entries, two closures - for one stretch of one pair in which
// you were in exactly once. The average on the header, which is the number this
// table exists for and the one maSweepPairs.pine sweeps, is fed both.
//
// THE RULE, in full:
//
//   Windows that OVERLAP on one symbol are one episode, and one episode is one
//   row. The survivor is the one whose WINDOW OPENED FIRST - the one you would
//   actually have been in - and it is kept WHOLE: its gate, its entries, its
//   side, its rung. Everything that started inside it is not recorded, and if a
//   closure arrives that started BEFORE rows already stored, those rows are
//   removed and it takes their place.
//
//   Ranked by the opening, DATED by the first entry. Two different questions,
//   and the row answers both: which series this was, and how long it held money.
//
//   Touching is not overlapping. A window that opens on the candle another
//   closed on is a second trade, because the first one had already paid.
//
// AND IT IS THE CHART'S RULE, exactly. mar1Gates.pine's leader claims the
// episode from the moment it opens and holds it until it finishes, and nothing
// that starts inside that episode reaches the candles at all. Drawn on the
// chart, row in the log; never drawn for its whole run, no row. The two are now
// the same sentence rather than two rules that happen to agree most of the time.
//
// The scan walks BACK from the newest row while its close is still after this
// window opened, which is the whole of the overlap test and terminates by
// itself: the array is pushed newest-last, so closes only decrease going back.
//
// More than one stored row can overlap a new closure even though no two stored
// rows overlap each other - two short windows an hour apart are both inside one
// long one that has only just finished - so this collects them all rather than
// stopping at the first.
// entryTimesStr arrives pre-built, one line already formatted by the caller
// (gateOne, which is the only place the per-entry timestamps live) - see
// entryTimes there. logClose only writes it into the Pine Logs line, gated on
// newWins so a window that loses the overlap dedup below is not logged twice
// under two different slots for the one episode it actually was.
logClose(int slot, int openMs, int rankMs, int entries, int dir, int step, string entryTimesStr) =>
    if time_close >= periodFrom and time_close < periodTo
        n = array.size(jClose)

        // The oldest row that still overlaps. Everything from here to the end
        // of the array is part of this episode.
        int first = n
        while first > 0 and array.get(jClose, first - 1) > rankMs
            first -= 1

        // The earliest opening among the rows already stored, and which row that
        // is. Walked oldest-first so a tie keeps the row that was there already.
        int bestStored = 0
        int keepIdx    = -1
        if first < n
            for k = first to n - 1
                r = array.get(jRank, k)
                if bestStored == 0 or r < bestStored
                    bestStored := r
                    keepIdx    := k

        // The new closure takes the episode only if it opened strictly before
        // every row it overlaps. On an EXACT tie - both windows opened on the
        // literal same bar, which happens whenever gate mode is Off or several
        // gates enter on the same rejection candle - the tie is broken exactly
        // the way the chart's own leader is: the HIGHER slot wins, D before C
        // before B before A and a gate's second length before its best. See
        // better() and its tie-break note for the reasoning - this dedup has to
        // keep the SAME series the chart is drawing, or the log and the candles
        // tell two different stories of the same morning.
        storedSlot = keepIdx >= 0 ? array.get(jGate, keepIdx) : -1
        newWins = keepIdx < 0 or rankMs < bestStored or (rankMs == bestStored and slot > storedSlot)

        // Removed from the BACK so the indices in front of each removal are
        // still the indices they were when they were found.
        if keepIdx >= 0
            for k = n - 1 to first
                if newWins or k != keepIdx
                    array.remove(jGate, k)
                    array.remove(jOpen, k)
                    array.remove(jRank, k)
                    array.remove(jClose, k)
                    array.remove(jEnt, k)
                    array.remove(jDir, k)
                    array.remove(jStep, k)

        if newWins
            array.push(jGate, slot)
            array.push(jOpen, openMs)
            array.push(jRank, rankMs)
            array.push(jClose, time_close)
            array.push(jEnt, entries)
            array.push(jDir, dir)
            array.push(jStep, step)

            if LOG_ENTRY_TIMES
                log.info('MAR1GATECLOSE;{0};{1};{2};{3};{4};{5}',
                     slotName(slot),
                     str.format_time(openMs, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'),
                     str.format_time(time_close, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'),
                     str.tostring(entries),
                     dir == 1 ? 'long' : 'short',
                     entryTimesStr)

            if array.size(jGate) > JRN_MAX
                array.shift(jGate)
                array.shift(jOpen)
                array.shift(jRank)
                array.shift(jClose)
                array.shift(jEnt)
                array.shift(jDir)
                array.shift(jStep)
    array.size(jGate)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One gate, one MA length
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// maRejection.pine's machine with the drawing, the statistics and the alerting
// lifted out, and with the two gate settings turned into arguments so eight copies
// can differ in them.
//
// It reads the shared block above straight out of the global scope rather than
// taking it as arguments - which maSweepPairs.pine could not do, because its
// copy runs inside request.security where the globals belong to another symbol.
// Here there is no security call and the globals are this chart's own.
//
// EIGHT CALL SITES ARE WHAT MAKE EIGHT MACHINES. A `var` inside a function
// belongs to the call site rather than to the function, so the histogram, the
// streak, the anchor and the window below are private to each of the eight. A loop
// would have given one machine run eight times and one answer.
//
// The block order is load-bearing and mirrors the order the original executes
// in: the gate reads the streak BEFORE this bar is graded, the markers read it
// AFTER. Swapping those is how a gate ends up judging a signal by its own
// result.
//
// Returns, in order: the four marker flags, the colour the marker is painted
// with, the streak, the threshold it is measured against, the anchor the ladder
// is drawn from, how many entries the open window has cost, winOpenAtSignal
// (the opening time this bar's signal should be judged by for leadership,
// captured before a win on this same bar can reset it to 0), and - last -
// seven raw ingredients the LIVE PREVIEW builds from outside this function:
// triggerLong, triggerShort, buyFound, sellFound, lastLongBar, lastShortBar and
// carriedAtSignal. buyFound / sellFound / lastLongBar / lastShortBar replace
// what used to be canFireLong / canFireShort outright - the preview needs the
// COOLDOWN PROJECTED one bar forward, the same way maRejection.pine's own does,
// and the raw cooldown flags could not do that on their own. See the note at
// liveCanLong below. They travel out raw rather than pre-combined into a live
// verdict because the live inputs - livePreview, liveFade - are declared at
// the foot of the file, after every call site below, and Pine will not let
// this function read an input that has not been declared yet. See livePrev()
// near THE EIGHT MARKS.
gateOne(simple int slot, simple int len, float pct, simple int minEnt) =>
    // switch, NOT the ?: chain this used to be. Pine evaluates EVERY branch of
    // a ternary, so the old spelling computed all four moving averages per
    // machine per bar and kept one. A switch runs only the selected branch,
    // and because maType is a simple input the SAME branch runs on every bar -
    // the series and its history are identical, only the three dead MAs are
    // gone. The compiler may warn about ta.* inside a conditional; that
    // warning is about conditions that change bar to bar, which this one
    // cannot.
    maValue = switch maType
        'WMA' => ta.wma(src, len)
        'EMA' => ta.ema(src, len)
        'RMA' => ta.rma(src, len)
        => ta.sma(src, len)

    // THE TREND TEST, AS A ROLLING SUM RATHER THAN A LOOP (2026-08-12). Same
    // test to the bit - not an approximation - and the same rewrite now lives
    // in maSweepPairs.pine and mar1GatesScanner.pine.
    //
    // It used to be `for i = 1 to trendBars`, reading atrVal[i], close[i] and
    // maValue[i] every iteration: nine history reads at a VARIABLE index per
    // machine per bar, eight machines deep. A variable index is the expensive
    // kind - Pine resolves a constant offset at compile time and cannot resolve
    // a loop counter, and has to keep a deeper buffer for every series read
    // that way.
    //
    // The identity: the loop's term at i is
    //
    //     close[i] <= maValue[i] - atrVal[i] * trendSep
    //
    // and every series in it is read at the SAME offset, so the whole term is
    // one expression evaluated i bars ago. The loop is therefore
    // math.sum(term, trendBars) shifted back one bar.
    //
    // The [1] is what makes the range 1..trendBars instead of 0..trendBars-1.
    // Drop it and the test starts including the bar being graded, which is a
    // different strategy.
    //
    // nz() covers the warmup, where math.sum is na until its window fills. The
    // loop reached past the start of history there and compared against na,
    // which is false and counted zero - nz gives the same zero and the same
    // false verdict, so nothing changes at the left edge of the chart.
    //
    // THE SMALLEST GAIN OF THE THREE FILES, and it is here for agreement rather
    // than for speed: eight machines on one chart is not what strains anything.
    // The signal block is meant to read identically wherever it appears, and a
    // file left on the old spelling is one somebody has to diff by hand later.
    // sepNow itself is global now - it does not depend on the MA, so it lives
    // with the rest of the shared block.
    belowNow = close <= maValue - sepNow ? 1 : 0
    aboveNow = close >= maValue + sepNow ? 1 : 0

    trendDown = nz(math.sum(belowNow, trendBars)[1]) == trendBars
    trendUp   = nz(math.sum(aboveNow, trendBars)[1]) == trendBars

    bodyRejectDown = allowBodyInside and open <= maValue and smallBody and upWick > bodySize
    bodyRejectUp   = allowBodyInside and open >= maValue and smallBody and dnWick > bodySize

    rejectDown = rangeSize > 0 and high >= maValue - tolVal and close <= maValue and (wickRejectDown or bodyRejectDown or longWickDown)
    rejectUp   = rangeSize > 0 and low  <= maValue + tolVal and close >= maValue and (wickRejectUp or bodyRejectUp or longWickUp)

    triggerShort = trendDown and (rejectDown or counterShort or dcShort)
    triggerLong  = trendUp and (rejectUp or counterLong or dcLong)

    var int lastLongBar  = -100000
    var int lastShortBar = -100000
    canFireLong  = bar_index - lastLongBar >= cooldown
    canFireShort = bar_index - lastShortBar >= cooldown

    // flatOk sits beside hoursOk because it is the same kind of veto - "not now"
    // - and it has to land in the same place: on the DETECTED signal, so that a
    // suppressed one never reaches the streak histogram below. See MODULE: flat
    // filter above. All eight machines read the one verdict: it is measured off
    // price alone, so there is nothing per-machine about it.
    buyFound  = triggerLong[1] and canFireLong and hoursOk and flatOk
    sellFound = triggerShort[1] and not triggerLong[1] and canFireShort and hoursOk and flatOk

    var int[] failHist    = array.new_int(16, 0)
    var int   curFailRun  = 0
    var int   streak      = 0
    var int   distCache   = -1
    var int   lockedLimit = 0

    gateThreshold = gateIsOff ? 0 :
         gateIsManual ? minEnt :
         gateIsDist ? (distCache > 0 ? distCache : minEnt) :
         math.max(distCache, minEnt)

    gateOk = streak >= gateThreshold

    // The anchor. Captured once while the streak reads zero and then held, so a
    // run climbs the ladder instead of chasing a threshold that a fresh failure
    // has just pushed up.
    if streak == 0
        lockedLimit := gateThreshold
    colorLimit = gateIsOff ? 0 : lockedLimit

    buyTaken     = buyFound and gateOk
    sellTaken    = sellFound and gateOk
    gateArmedNow = not gateIsOff and streak == gateThreshold - 1
    buyWarn      = buyFound and gateArmedNow
    sellWarn     = sellFound and gateArmedNow

    // Read BEFORE the grading below moves either of them. This is the whole of
    // what makes the ladder never repeat a colour: a marker inherits the exact
    // colour of the failure marker before it, and only computes a fresh one when
    // no run is in progress.
    var color carriedColor = na
    streakAtSignal  = streak
    carriedAtSignal = carriedColor

    var int  entriesInWindow = 0
    var bool windowOpen      = false

    // What the RUNNING table needs and the markers cannot say: when this window
    // started, and when the last signal on this gate was. Both are timestamps of
    // the CANDLE, not of the bar the marker was drawn on, so they line up with
    // the times in the closed log.
    var int  winOpenTime = 0
    var int  lastSigTime = 0
    var int  lastSigDir  = 0

    // Snapshotted from winOpenTime after the entry block below and before the
    // win-reset can zero it - see the note at that assignment. Declared as a
    // `var` up here rather than as a plain local down there because the return
    // tuple at the foot of this function needs it, and a local inside `if
    // barstate.isconfirmed` goes out of scope the moment that block ends.
    var int  winOpenAtSignal = 0

    // WHEN MONEY FIRST WENT IN, kept apart from winOpenTime because the two
    // answer different questions and their answers differ by however long the
    // gate stood open before anything was entered - which can be a quarter of an
    // hour.
    //
    //   winOpenTime    when the GATE opened. The running table's question:
    //                  since when has this slot been demanding attention.
    //   firstEntTime   when the SERIES started costing. The log's question, and
    //                  the one maRejection.pine's success stamp already answers
    //                  with windowEntryTime - which is what lets a row here and a
    //                  stamp there be recognised as the same series.
    //
    // mar1Scanner.pine keeps the same pair, the same way round, for the same
    // reason. Dating the log from the gate instead is what made the gates scanner
    // print 13:49 where mar1Scanner printed 14:03 on one win of one pair on
    // identical settings.
    var int  firstEntTime = 0

    // Every entry this window has cost so far, in order - the raw material
    // for the per-entry Pine Logs line (user request, 2026-08-11): not just
    // when the episode opened and closed, but when the second entry landed,
    // the third, and so on. Pushed on every entryTaken below, formatted into
    // one string right before logClose() is called, and cleared the instant
    // the window's life ends - on a win, where it has just been read, and on
    // greenDiamond, defensively, though a fresh window should never find it
    // non-empty in practice (see the note at greenDiamond's reset below).
    var int[] entryTimes = array.new_int(0)

    if barstate.isconfirmed
        if buyFound
            lastLongBar := bar_index
            if not perDirectionCooldown
                lastShortBar := bar_index
        if sellFound
            lastShortBar := bar_index
            if not perDirectionCooldown
                lastLongBar := bar_index

        // Doji is a miss on both sides, matching the original exactly.
        if bodyRefReady and (buyFound or sellFound)
            hit = (buyFound and isBull) or (sellFound and isBear)
            histChanged = false

            if hit
                curFailRun := 0
            else
                curFailRun += 1
                // ONCE A STREAK REACHES THE CATCH-ALL, NOTHING MOVES ANY MORE - and this
                // used to keep moving it, which is the bug this guard fixes rather than a
                // style choice.
                //
                // The transfer is what keeps a LIVE streak in exactly one bucket: as it grows
                // past n it is added to bucket n and taken back out of n-1. Bucket 15 is a
                // catch-all, so a streak that reaches 15 has ARRIVED and never leaves. With the
                // index clamped by math.min, the sixteenth failure ran the transfer AGAIN -
                // adding a second copy of one live streak to bucket 15 and taking a real one
                // OUT of bucket 14, which went NEGATIVE by one per extra failure.
                //
                // THE TOTAL WAS NEVER AFFECTED, and that is what made it silent for as long as
                // it was: the +1 and the -1 both sit inside the summed range 1..15, so the one
                // number anybody could have checked by hand stayed exactly right while the
                // shape underneath it did not.
                //
                // What it cost: calcIndex walks the cumulative distribution and takes the FIRST
                // crossing of the percentage. A negative bucket 14 drags that cumulative back
                // DOWN, so a crossing that belonged at 14 was missed and picked up at 15
                // instead - one rung too high on the gate threshold, in Distribution and in Max
                // of both. The gate then opens one loss LATER than the distribution says it
                // should. The error is one-sided and bounded: never earlier, and never any
                // bucket other than 14 -> 15.
                //
                // Worked example, 20 consecutive misses against 19 shorter streaks at 95%:
                // bucket 14 reads -4 and bucket 15 reads 6 where they should read 1 and 1, the
                // cumulative falls from 90% to 70% at bucket 14, and the answer comes back 15
                // instead of 14.
                //
                // The same five lines live in SIX files. maRejection.pine is the one where this
                // feeds the gate that decides real entries.
                if curFailRun <= 15
                    array.set(failHist, curFailRun, array.get(failHist, curFailRun) + 1)
                    array.set(failHist, curFailRun - 1, array.get(failHist, curFailRun - 1) - 1)
                    histChanged := true

            streak := hit ? 0 : streak + 1
            // Recomputed only when the histogram itself moved - on a hit, and
            // on a miss already past the catch-all bucket, calcIndex would
            // return exactly what distCache already holds. gateNeedsDist skips
            // it in Off/Manual, where distCache goes stale but nothing reads
            // it - if a future mode starts reading it there, drop gateNeedsDist
            // from this test.
            if histChanged and gateNeedsDist
                distCache := calcIndex(failHist, pct)

        // Ladder bookkeeping, deliberately after the grading so the streak it
        // reads already includes this signal's own result. A marker that failed
        // stores its colour; a marker that won clears the store.
        if buyTaken or sellTaken or buyWarn or sellWarn
            markerMissed = (buyTaken or buyWarn) and not isBull or (sellTaken or sellWarn) and not isBear
            carriedColor := markerMissed ? streakColor(streak, colorLimit) : na

        buyFailed    = (buyTaken or buyWarn) and not isBull
        sellFailed   = (sellTaken or sellWarn) and not isBear
        greenDiamond = (buyFailed or sellFailed) and colorLimit > 0 and streak == colorLimit

        // Which successes close a window, exactly as the scanner reads it.
        buySet = scopeAllFound ? buyFound :
             scopeWarns ? (buyTaken or buyWarn) :
             buyTaken
        sellSet = scopeAllFound ? sellFound :
             scopeWarns ? (sellTaken or sellWarn) :
             sellTaken

        win        = (buySet and isBull) or (sellSet and isBear)
        entryTaken = buyTaken or sellTaken

        if buyTaken or sellTaken or buyWarn or sellWarn
            lastSigTime := time
            lastSigDir  := buyTaken or buyWarn ? 1 : -1

        if entryTaken
            entriesInWindow += 1
            array.push(entryTimes, time)
            // Only the first one. The row reports the span of the whole series,
            // and every entry after the first is inside a series that already has
            // a start.
            if entriesInWindow == 1
                firstEntTime := time
            if not windowOpen
                windowOpen := true
                // With the streak gate Off there is no green diamond to open a
                // window, so the first entry is the opening. Timed here rather
                // than at the diamond for exactly that case.
                winOpenTime := time

        // CAPTURED HERE, before a win below can erase it. winOpenTime is reset
        // to 0 the instant a window wins - entriesInWindow and firstEntTime go
        // with it - because a closed window has nothing left to report to the
        // RUNNING table. But the WINNING entry's own leadership claim is judged
        // from this exact bar, using this exact timestamp: a gate that has been
        // leading for an hour and wins now must not lose that claim to a gate
        // that opened five minutes ago, on the one bar where it matters most.
        // Reading winOpenTime after the reset returned 0, which made the
        // winning entry fall back to `time` - this bar - and a gate that
        // opened five minutes ago beat it on "earliest wins". The mark that
        // should have been the easiest of all to justify, the one that just
        // paid, was the one that vanished. See winOpenAtSignal in the return
        // tuple and a1EffOpnT and its seven siblings below.
        winOpenAtSignal := winOpenTime

        if win
            // A window that closed without ever having been opened by a gate or
            // an entry is not a window - it is a win on a pair that was flat, and
            // logging it would put a zero-entry row in a log about cost.
            //
            // The fallback chain is for the window that closed without ever
            // entering - a gate opened on a diamond and the very next signal won.
            // It has no first entry to be dated from.
            // AND ONLY IF THE GATE IS TICKED. Everything else this machine can
            // put on screen already dies with its tick box; the log was the one
            // output written from inside here, and it went on recording. tfOk is
            // in the same test for the same reason - the marks are silent on a
            // timeframe the grid has no lengths for, so the log must be too, or
            // it fills up with windows measured on lengths chosen elsewhere.
            if (windowOpen or entriesInWindow > 0) and slotOn(slot) and tfOk
                logClose(slot, firstEntTime > 0 ? firstEntTime : (winOpenTime == 0 ? time : winOpenTime),
                     winOpenTime == 0 ? time : winOpenTime,
                     entriesInWindow, buySet and isBull ? 1 : -1, ladderRung(entriesInWindow), joinTimes(entryTimes))
            array.clear(entryTimes)
            windowOpen      := false
            entriesInWindow := 0
            winOpenTime     := 0
            firstEntTime    := 0
        else if greenDiamond
            // Defensive rather than load-bearing: a window's entryTimes is
            // already emptied the instant it wins, above, and greenDiamond can
            // only fire once per losing streak - see the note at colorLimit -
            // so a fresh window should never find this non-empty. Cleared
            // anyway, so a future change to that invariant fails safe rather
            // than silently carrying yesterday's entries into today's episode.
            array.clear(entryTimes)
            windowOpen      := true
            entriesInWindow := 0
            winOpenTime     := time
            firstEntTime    := 0

        // A periodic line for whatever is still open - the other half of the
        // per-entry export above (user request, 2026-08-11): the closed log
        // only ever shows an episode AFTER it pays, so anything still running
        // had nothing here to read at all. This is that reading, available
        // without waiting for a win that may be hours away - "не тільки
        // закриті, а й ще відкриті" (not only closed, but the still-open ones
        // too).
        //
        // AT THE LIVE EDGE ONLY (2026-08-12), matching the identical guard in
        // mar1GatesScanner.pine - see the long note there for the reasoning.
        // In short: this used to write one line per confirmed bar per open
        // window, each one building a string through joinTimes, and every one
        // of those lines was superseded by the next bar the moment it
        // confirmed. A finished episode is already in MAR1GATECLOSE with its
        // full entry list, so the surviving line is the only one that was ever
        // read. The two files must keep the same rule or a log pasted out of
        // one cannot be read beside a log pasted out of the other.
        //
        // windowOpen alone, not entriesInWindow > 0 - a gate ARMED with nothing
        // entered yet is still "not closed" in the literal sense that phrase
        // was asked for, and entries reads 0 there honestly rather than being
        // excluded from the line.
        //
        // Runs AFTER the win / greenDiamond block above, so a window that just
        // closed this exact bar is correctly absent here - it was written as
        // MAR1GATECLOSE instead, once, not written twice under two names.
        liveEdge = barstate.islastconfirmedhistory or barstate.isrealtime

        if windowOpen and LOG_ENTRY_TIMES and slotOn(slot) and tfOk and liveEdge
            log.info('MAR1GATEOPEN;{0};{1};{2};{3}',
                 slotName(slot),
                 str.format_time(firstEntTime > 0 ? firstEntTime : winOpenTime, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'),
                 str.tostring(entriesInWindow),
                 joinTimes(entryTimes))

        // The session-boundary line - a window still open exactly when the
        // hours filter's session ends gets logged as if it closed there, close
        // time set to this bar's own time (user request, 2026-08-11: "якщо
        // сесія виконання вказана" - only when restrictToHours actually names
        // one, which sessionJustEnded already requires).
        //
        // LOG ONLY. Nothing below this line touches windowOpen, entriesInWindow,
        // firstEntTime or entryTimes - the machine itself does not learn the
        // session ended and carries the same open window into tomorrow exactly
        // as it always has, restrictToHours alone still deciding whether a new
        // signal can fire in the meantime. This is a second, independent
        // bookkeeping line for the analytics reading the log, not a second
        // closure the chart or the closed-log arrays know anything about.
        if sessionJustEnded and windowOpen and LOG_ENTRY_TIMES and slotOn(slot) and tfOk
            log.info('MAR1GATESESSIONCLOSE;{0};{1};{2};{3};{4}',
                 slotName(slot),
                 str.format_time(firstEntTime > 0 ? firstEntTime : winOpenTime, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'),
                 str.format_time(time, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1'),
                 str.tostring(entriesInWindow),
                 joinTimes(entryTimes))

    markColor = na(carriedAtSignal) ? streakColor(streakAtSignal, colorLimit) : carriedAtSignal

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The two SMALL markers, which this file did not draw and had to
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // A large diamond says a marker happened. It cannot say whether it PAID,
    // and that is the whole question a recovery ladder turns on. maRejection.pine
    // answers it with a tiny diamond on the candle that failed; eight machines on
    // one chart need the same answer eight times or the chart shows entries with
    // no outcomes and the scanner looks like it is describing another symbol.
    //
    // THE FAILURE DIAMOND. Drawn when a large marker did not go its way, in the
    // colour of the streak WITH this candle's result already folded in - which
    // is why it is read here, after the grading block above, and not beside the
    // large marker's own colour. A candle with a large diamond and no small one
    // under it is a marker that won, and a win closes the window: the ladder
    // stops there and the next run starts from the bottom.
    //
    // Gated on a confirmed bar because this file draws no intrabar previews. On
    // the candle still forming the streak has not been graded yet, so the marker
    // would appear in the previous rung's colour and change shade at the close -
    // the exact flicker maRejection.pine had to build a projection to remove.
    markerFailed = barstate.isconfirmed and
         ((buyTaken or buyWarn) and not isBull or (sellTaken or sellWarn) and not isBear)
    failedLong  = markerFailed and (buyTaken or buyWarn)
    failedShort = markerFailed and (sellTaken or sellWarn)
    failColor   = streakColor(streak, colorLimit)

    // THE WHITE STEP. The moment the ladder first reaches one short of the gate,
    // which is the state the scanner paints a white cell for. Without it the two
    // could not be reconciled: the scanner said pre-armed and the chart said
    // nothing, because a gate standing one step away is not an event and nothing
    // was drawing states.
    //
    // A signal on this bar is required, and not as a formality. The streak only
    // moves when a signal is graded, but colorLimit can move on its own when the
    // anchor is re-captured - and without this the step could appear to be
    // entered on a candle where nothing happened at all, putting a marker
    // somewhere the eye has no way to explain.
    var bool onWhiteStep  = false
    var bool whiteStepHit = false

    // WHEN this gate arrived on the white step, and held until it leaves. A gate
    // standing one short of its anchor has no window, so it has no winOpenTime -
    // and it is still the first thing this machine did. Without a stamp of its
    // own, a pre-armed gate could not be ranked against anything and every mark
    // that landed while nothing was open came out at full strength.
    //
    // Set on ARRIVAL and held: the second visit to this line during the same
    // wait must not restamp it, or "since when" would always read "just now".
    var int whiteSince = 0

    if barstate.isconfirmed
        nowWhite = colorLimit > 0 and streak == colorLimit - 1
        whiteStepHit := nowWhite and not onWhiteStep and (buyFound or sellFound)
        onWhiteStep  := nowWhite
        whiteSince   := nowWhite ? (whiteSince == 0 ? time : whiteSince) : 0

    // Side by the signal that did it, the same rule the failure diamonds use.
    // barstate.isconfirmed again because whiteStepHit is a var and holds its last
    // value across the ticks of an unfinished candle.
    whiteLong  = whiteStepHit and barstate.isconfirmed and buyFound
    whiteShort = whiteStepHit and barstate.isconfirmed and not buyFound

    [buyTaken, sellTaken, buyWarn, sellWarn, markColor, streakAtSignal, gateThreshold, colorLimit, entriesInWindow,
     windowOpen, winOpenTime, lastSigTime, lastSigDir, failedLong, failedShort, failColor, whiteLong, whiteShort, firstEntTime, whiteSince, winOpenAtSignal,
     triggerLong, triggerShort, buyFound, sellFound, lastLongBar, lastShortBar, carriedAtSignal]

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The eight
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Two per gate: the length the sweep ranked first, and the one it ranked second.
// The tick boxes only silence the OUTPUT - the machine behind an unticked slot
// still runs, because a call site cannot be made conditional in Pine. Untick to
// clear the chart, not to make it faster.
[a1Buy, a1Sell, a1BuyW, a1SellW, a1Col, a1Streak, a1Thr, a1Lim, a1Win, a1Open, a1OpenT, a1LastT, a1LastD, a1FailL, a1FailS, a1FailC, a1WhL, a1WhS, a1FeT, a1WhT, a1SigT, a1TrL, a1TrS, a1Bf, a1Sf, a1LLB, a1LSB, a1CarC] = gateOne(0, lenA1, pctA, minA)
[a2Buy, a2Sell, a2BuyW, a2SellW, a2Col, a2Streak, a2Thr, a2Lim, a2Win, a2Open, a2OpenT, a2LastT, a2LastD, a2FailL, a2FailS, a2FailC, a2WhL, a2WhS, a2FeT, a2WhT, a2SigT, a2TrL, a2TrS, a2Bf, a2Sf, a2LLB, a2LSB, a2CarC] = gateOne(1, lenA2, pctA, minA)
[b1Buy, b1Sell, b1BuyW, b1SellW, b1Col, b1Streak, b1Thr, b1Lim, b1Win, b1Open, b1OpenT, b1LastT, b1LastD, b1FailL, b1FailS, b1FailC, b1WhL, b1WhS, b1FeT, b1WhT, b1SigT, b1TrL, b1TrS, b1Bf, b1Sf, b1LLB, b1LSB, b1CarC] = gateOne(2, lenB1, pctB, minB)
[b2Buy, b2Sell, b2BuyW, b2SellW, b2Col, b2Streak, b2Thr, b2Lim, b2Win, b2Open, b2OpenT, b2LastT, b2LastD, b2FailL, b2FailS, b2FailC, b2WhL, b2WhS, b2FeT, b2WhT, b2SigT, b2TrL, b2TrS, b2Bf, b2Sf, b2LLB, b2LSB, b2CarC] = gateOne(3, lenB2, pctB, minB)
[c1Buy, c1Sell, c1BuyW, c1SellW, c1Col, c1Streak, c1Thr, c1Lim, c1Win, c1Open, c1OpenT, c1LastT, c1LastD, c1FailL, c1FailS, c1FailC, c1WhL, c1WhS, c1FeT, c1WhT, c1SigT, c1TrL, c1TrS, c1Bf, c1Sf, c1LLB, c1LSB, c1CarC] = gateOne(4, lenC1, pctC, minC)
[c2Buy, c2Sell, c2BuyW, c2SellW, c2Col, c2Streak, c2Thr, c2Lim, c2Win, c2Open, c2OpenT, c2LastT, c2LastD, c2FailL, c2FailS, c2FailC, c2WhL, c2WhS, c2FeT, c2WhT, c2SigT, c2TrL, c2TrS, c2Bf, c2Sf, c2LLB, c2LSB, c2CarC] = gateOne(5, lenC2, pctC, minC)
[d1Buy, d1Sell, d1BuyW, d1SellW, d1Col, d1Streak, d1Thr, d1Lim, d1Win, d1Open, d1OpenT, d1LastT, d1LastD, d1FailL, d1FailS, d1FailC, d1WhL, d1WhS, d1FeT, d1WhT, d1SigT, d1TrL, d1TrS, d1Bf, d1Sf, d1LLB, d1LSB, d1CarC] = gateOne(6, lenD1, pctD, minD)
[d2Buy, d2Sell, d2BuyW, d2SellW, d2Col, d2Streak, d2Thr, d2Lim, d2Win, d2Open, d2OpenT, d2LastT, d2LastD, d2FailL, d2FailS, d2FailC, d2WhL, d2WhS, d2FeT, d2WhT, d2SigT, d2TrL, d2TrS, d2Bf, d2Sf, d2LLB, d2LSB, d2CarC] = gateOne(7, lenD2, pctD, minD)

// Ageing out, from the front, which is where the oldest row is - the log is
// pushed newest-last and read backwards.
//
// It exists for the LIVE bar and nothing else. periodFrom is built from timenow,
// which is frozen for the whole of one historical pass, so on that pass the
// filter inside logClose already keeps the arrays clean. On a running chart
// timenow advances, a rolling window's start moves with it, and rows that were
// legitimately recorded a minute ago fall out the back. A while loop covers both
// kinds of setting with the same two lines: a rolling window drops one row at a
// time, and a calendar one empties the whole list at once when midnight moves
// the cutoff past every row in it.
if barstate.isconfirmed
    while array.size(jClose) > 0 and array.get(jClose, 0) < periodFrom
        array.shift(jGate)
        array.shift(jOpen)
        array.shift(jRank)
        array.shift(jClose)
        array.shift(jEnt)
        array.shift(jDir)
        array.shift(jStep)

// A slot draws only when its gate is ticked AND the chart is on a timeframe the
// grid has lengths for. Both halves of that are in one place so no plot can
// disagree with another about when it is allowed to speak.
liveA = onA and tfOk
liveB = onB and tfOk
liveC = onC and tfOk
liveD = onD and tfOk

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE LEADING SERIES - which of the eight has been carrying money longest
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Eight machines run on one symbol and several of them can be in a series at the
// same moment. The scanner's closed log keeps ONE row for them - the series that
// ran longest - and this is the same choice made a bar at a time, so the chart
// and that row are about the same machine rather than two accounts of one
// morning that have to be reconciled by eye.
//
// ONLY THE LEADER'S MARKER DRAWS. This used to wash the other seven to 90
// instead of hiding them, on the grounds that a signal that happened is a fact
// and a chart that suppresses facts is worse than a busy one. That trade was
// reversed on request: eight machines drawing on one symbol, most of them
// pale, was still eight shapes and eight captions computed and placed on every
// bar, and the point of running one gate at a time on a chart is to see the one
// gate that is actually carrying the episode - not to be shown, faintly, that
// seven others also had an opinion. The tables still carry the full account for
// every gate whether it is leading or not; only the CANDLES now show one.
//
// MEASURED FROM THE WINDOW OPENING - the GREEN DIAMOND - and not from the first
// entry. This was the other way round and it was wrong, in a way that showed on
// the chart as the exact fault it was meant to prevent:
//
//   A green diamond RESETS the entry count. It is a taken entry that failed
//   exactly on the anchor, and the machine treats it as the start of a fresh
//   recovery window - entriesInWindow goes back to zero and firstEntTime with
//   it. So for as long as a gate stood open on its diamond and had not entered
//   again, it had no entries and no first entry, was no candidate to lead, and
//   NOTHING led. leadS stayed -1, dimOff was false for all eight, and a sun
//   and a flag firing two candles later came out at FULL STRENGTH beside a green
//   diamond that had opened before either of them.
//
// The window opening is the honest start of a series. It is what the green
// diamond marks, what the RUNNING table dates its rows from, and what the eye
// reads as "this one is running".
//
// AND A PRE-ARMED GATE COUNTS TOO, which is the second half of the same fault.
// A gate standing one short of its anchor has no window open, so under the rule
// above it could not lead - and while NOTHING led, dimOff was false for all
// eight and every mark came out at full strength. That is how a white flag on
// gate B landed at full colour beside a green diamond on gate A that had marked
// the same episode two candles earlier.
//
// So there are TWO TIERS, and the tier is read before the clock:
//
//   2  a window is OPEN        ranked by when it opened
//   1  standing PRE-ARMED      ranked by when it arrived on the white step
//   0  nothing                 no claim at all
//
// The tier comes first on purpose. A gate that has been pre-armed since the
// morning must not take the lead from a window that opened ten minutes ago and
// is four entries deep - a run that is costing money outranks a gate that is
// merely worth watching, whatever the clock says. Within a tier the earliest
// wins, and that is the rule as stated: whoever was there first.
//
// A slot is a candidate only if its gate is TICKED. An unticked gate draws
// nothing, so letting one lead would hide the whole chart on behalf of a
// machine with no marks on it.
//
// Ties keep the HIGHEST slot - D before C before B before A, and inside a gate
// the second length before the best. Two claims that began on the same candle
// have no honest ranking by the clock alone, so the tie is broken by how tight
// the gate is: D is the tightest of the four, so a claim that survived D's
// stricter percentage is trusted over one that only had to clear A's, and the
// second length inside a gate is the CONTROL - see the header - so it stands
// behind its own best length everywhere except this one tie-break, where the
// two are equally valid claims and the higher slot number is the one kept.
// This is deliberately the OPPOSITE of "first in the dialog wins": the dialog
// lists A before D because that is the order the settings were swept in, not
// because A's claim is worth more when two windows close identically on the
// same candle.
//
// t <= bestT, not t < bestT, is the entire mechanism. The eight calls below
// run slot 0 to slot 7 in order, so on a genuine tie every later slot's
// timestamp is EQUAL to the one already held, not smaller - a strict `<` would
// never fire again once slot 0 claimed it, and the lowest slot would win every
// tie by default. `<=` lets each later slot in the chain overwrite an exact
// tie, so the LAST slot standing - the highest one that was tied - is what
// survives to the end of the chain. A genuinely EARLIER timestamp still wins
// outright, because `<=` still fires for it; only an EQUAL one falls through
// to this rule.
better(int bestT, int bestS, int bestTier, bool on, bool opn, bool pre, int tOpen, int tWhite, int slot) =>
    int  rt   = bestT
    int  rs   = bestS
    int  rr   = bestTier
    tier = opn ? 2 : pre ? 1 : 0
    t    = opn ? tOpen : tWhite
    if on and tier > 0 and t > 0 and (bestS < 0 or tier > bestTier or (tier == bestTier and t <= bestT))
        rt := t
        rs := slot
        rr := tier
    [rt, rs, rr]

// Pre-armed is read from the streak against the anchor, the same test the
// RUNNING table uses for its palest row - one short of the gate.
preA1 = a1Lim > 0 and a1Streak == a1Lim - 1
preA2 = a2Lim > 0 and a2Streak == a2Lim - 1
preB1 = b1Lim > 0 and b1Streak == b1Lim - 1
preB2 = b2Lim > 0 and b2Streak == b2Lim - 1
preC1 = c1Lim > 0 and c1Streak == c1Lim - 1
preC2 = c2Lim > 0 and c2Streak == c2Lim - 1
preD1 = d1Lim > 0 and d1Streak == d1Lim - 1
preD2 = d2Lim > 0 and d2Streak == d2Lim - 1

// THE ONE-TICK LAG THIS CLOSES. a1Open (windowOpen) and a1WhT (whiteSince) are
// only written inside gateOne's `if barstate.isconfirmed` block, so on the bar
// where a signal FIRST fires they are still whatever they were at the END of
// the LAST CONFIRMED bar - they have not caught up to the entry or warning
// that just happened on THIS tick. buyTaken/sellTaken/buyWarn/sellWarn, by
// contrast, are plain, unconditional expressions and are already true the
// moment the signal fires.
//
// Feeding the raw a1Open/preA1 into better() means: on a bar where SEVERAL
// gates fire their FIRST entry or warning at once - one strong rejection
// candle satisfies more than one MA length - and NONE of them was already
// leading, every one of them looks like tier 0 to better() for that entire
// tick, because none has been stamped yet. leadS stays -1, dimOff refuses no
// one, and every one of those gates draws. It self-corrects the instant the
// bar confirms and gateOne's own state catches up - which is why this only
// ever shows up on the live, still-forming bar and never on settled history -
// but for as long as the bar is open, the pile-up is real.
//
// The fix closes the gap rather than waiting it out: a slot counts as tier 2
// the moment its entry FIRES, not only once windowOpen has been written, and
// the same for tier 1 against a warning. The provisional timestamp is `time` -
// this bar's open - which is exactly what winOpenTime/whiteSince WILL be
// stamped as once the bar confirms, so a tie between several gates firing on
// the same tick resolves by the same "lower slot wins" rule it would have
// resolved by anyway, just one tick sooner. An already-open window keeps its
// OWN earlier anchor rather than being reset to `time` by a later entry in the
// same series.
//
// A SECOND GAP OF THE SAME SHAPE sits on the OTHER end of a window's life, and
// it is the one that made a WINNING entry disappear rather than a losing one.
// gateOne resets winOpenTime to 0 in the SAME bar a window wins - entriesInWindow
// and firstEntTime go with it, because a closed window has nothing left to
// report to the RUNNING table. But that reset happens BEFORE this bar's
// leadership is judged, so a1OpenT was already 0 by the time it got here: a
// gate that had been leading for an hour and won on this exact bar fell back to
// `time` - as if its window had only just opened - and lost the tie to any gate
// that had genuinely opened more recently. The mark that should have been the
// easiest of all to justify, the one that just paid, was the one dimOff hid.
//
// winOpenAtSignal (aXSigT below) is gateOne's fix for this half: captured
// straight after the entry block and before the win-reset, so it carries the
// window's TRUE opening time on the bar it wins, and only falls back to `time`
// when there genuinely is no prior opening to report - a fresh window, entered
// and won on the same bar. Used here instead of aXOpen/aXOpenT for exactly that
// reason: aXOpen has already gone false and aXOpenT already 0 by the time either
// is read on a winning bar, and only aXSigT still remembers what they were.
a1EffOpn  = a1Open or a1Buy or a1Sell
a1EffOpnT = a1SigT > 0 ? a1SigT : time
a1EffPre  = preA1 or a1BuyW or a1SellW
a1EffPreT = preA1 ? a1WhT : time

a2EffOpn  = a2Open or a2Buy or a2Sell
a2EffOpnT = a2SigT > 0 ? a2SigT : time
a2EffPre  = preA2 or a2BuyW or a2SellW
a2EffPreT = preA2 ? a2WhT : time

b1EffOpn  = b1Open or b1Buy or b1Sell
b1EffOpnT = b1SigT > 0 ? b1SigT : time
b1EffPre  = preB1 or b1BuyW or b1SellW
b1EffPreT = preB1 ? b1WhT : time

b2EffOpn  = b2Open or b2Buy or b2Sell
b2EffOpnT = b2SigT > 0 ? b2SigT : time
b2EffPre  = preB2 or b2BuyW or b2SellW
b2EffPreT = preB2 ? b2WhT : time

c1EffOpn  = c1Open or c1Buy or c1Sell
c1EffOpnT = c1SigT > 0 ? c1SigT : time
c1EffPre  = preC1 or c1BuyW or c1SellW
c1EffPreT = preC1 ? c1WhT : time

c2EffOpn  = c2Open or c2Buy or c2Sell
c2EffOpnT = c2SigT > 0 ? c2SigT : time
c2EffPre  = preC2 or c2BuyW or c2SellW
c2EffPreT = preC2 ? c2WhT : time

d1EffOpn  = d1Open or d1Buy or d1Sell
d1EffOpnT = d1SigT > 0 ? d1SigT : time
d1EffPre  = preD1 or d1BuyW or d1SellW
d1EffPreT = preD1 ? d1WhT : time

d2EffOpn  = d2Open or d2Buy or d2Sell
d2EffOpnT = d2SigT > 0 ? d2SigT : time
d2EffPre  = preD2 or d2BuyW or d2SellW
d2EffPreT = preD2 ? d2WhT : time

[lt1, ls1, lr1] = better(0, -1, 0, liveA, a1EffOpn, a1EffPre, a1EffOpnT, a1EffPreT, 0)
[lt2, ls2, lr2] = better(lt1, ls1, lr1, liveA, a2EffOpn, a2EffPre, a2EffOpnT, a2EffPreT, 1)
[lt3, ls3, lr3] = better(lt2, ls2, lr2, liveB, b1EffOpn, b1EffPre, b1EffOpnT, b1EffPreT, 2)
[lt4, ls4, lr4] = better(lt3, ls3, lr3, liveB, b2EffOpn, b2EffPre, b2EffOpnT, b2EffPreT, 3)
[lt5, ls5, lr5] = better(lt4, ls4, lr4, liveC, c1EffOpn, c1EffPre, c1EffOpnT, c1EffPreT, 4)
[lt6, ls6, lr6] = better(lt5, ls5, lr5, liveC, c2EffOpn, c2EffPre, c2EffOpnT, c2EffPreT, 5)
[lt7, ls7, lr7] = better(lt6, ls6, lr6, liveD, d1EffOpn, d1EffPre, d1EffOpnT, d1EffPreT, 6)
[leadT, leadS, leadR] = better(lt7, ls7, lr7, liveD, d2EffOpn, d2EffPre, d2EffOpnT, d2EffPreT, 7)

// Nothing running means nothing to lead, and then NOTHING is hidden. A chart
// where every marker is suppressed because no series is open would be saying
// "none of these matters", which is the opposite of true.
dimOff(int slot) => leadS >= 0 and leadS != slot

// A non-leader's mark washes to 90% instead of being suppressed outright
// (user rule, 2026-08-10 - reverts "only the leader draws" back to the
// washed version this file used before that change, on the same grounds
// the original wash existed for: a signal that happened is a fact, and a
// chart that hides it entirely reads as though it never happened at all).
washCol(color col, int slot) => dimOff(slot) ? color.new(col, 90) : col

// WHERE A MACHINE STANDS, counted outwards from the candle. The leader is pulled
// to the front row and everything else queues behind it in slot order, so no two
// marks can land on the same price and reading outwards from the candle is
// reading in importance order.
//
// THE LANE IS DENSE, and that is the whole of this function. It counts only the
// machines that are ACTUALLY DRAWING on this bar, not the slot number.
//
// It was `leadS == slot ? 0 : slot + 1` - one reserved lane per slot, always
// eight of them - and that is why a lone gate-D mark floated eight ATR above the
// candle with seven empty lanes underneath it. Nothing was in those lanes; they
// were reserved for machines that had said nothing. A mark that far from its own
// candle stops pointing at the candle, which is the one thing it exists to do.
//
// Now a bar with two marks uses lanes 0 and 1 and a bar with one uses lane 0,
// whichever slots they happen to be. The separation is still exact - two marks
// only ever share a lane if one of them is not drawn - and the height is the
// height the bar actually needs.
//
// The eight flags come in as arguments rather than being read from the global
// scope, because the two marker families fire on DIFFERENT bars: an entry is on
// the rejection candle and its outcome is on the candle that graded it. One
// shared lane table would put an outcome in a lane sized for the entries.
//
// `if slot > 0` is not defensive. Pine's `for t = 0 to -1` counts DOWN and runs
// twice, so on slot 0 the loop would read index -1 and kill the script.
lane(int slot, bool f0, bool f1, bool f2, bool f3, bool f4, bool f5, bool f6, bool f7) =>
    // AND THE LEADER'S FRONT ROW IS DENSE TOO, which it was not. `n := 1` held
    // lane 0 for the leading slot on EVERY bar - including the overwhelming
    // majority of bars where the leader says nothing at all. A leader is a slot
    // that opened a window ten candles ago and is still running; it draws a mark
    // on the candles it actually signals on, and on all the others its lane sat
    // empty with the whole stack standing behind it.
    //
    // leadS == -1 was the same fault at its worst: nothing leading meant nothing
    // dimmed, and every one of the eight still started at lane 1, so a bar with
    // ONE mark on it put that mark a full lane out for the benefit of nobody.
    leadDraws = leadS < 0 ? false :
         leadS == 0 ? f0 : leadS == 1 ? f1 : leadS == 2 ? f2 : leadS == 3 ? f3 :
         leadS == 4 ? f4 : leadS == 5 ? f5 : leadS == 6 ? f6 : f7
    int n = 0
    if leadS != slot
        n := leadDraws ? 1 : 0
        if slot > 0
            for t = 0 to slot - 1
                ft = t == 0 ? f0 : t == 1 ? f1 : t == 2 ? f2 : t == 3 ? f3 :
                     t == 4 ? f4 : t == 5 ? f5 : t == 6 ? f6 : f7
                if t != leadS and ft
                    n += 1
    n

// Entries and warnings share a marker, exactly as they do in maRejection.pine,
// and they are still told apart at a glance: a warning can only arrive one step
// below the anchor, which is the white rung, and an entry can only arrive on the
// anchor or above it, which is lime and up. The colour separates them; a second
// shape would only have said the same thing twice.
// The flags themselves are built at the foot of the file, where the marks are
// drawn - there is no intermediate name for them here any more, because a plot
// declaration needed one and a label call does not.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The markers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GLYPH IS GLUED TO THE LETTER, not to the percentage / min-entries pair. A
// gate's numbers can be edited freely - resweeps, retuning, a fresh set of
// four - and its shape never moves, because the shape is chosen by the SLOT
// (A/B/C/D), not by what that slot currently reads.
//
// THE COST OF THAT CHOICE, stated plainly: a screenshot taken before a gate's
// numbers were last edited shows the right shape meaning the wrong numbers, and
// there is nothing in the file that can warn about that - the marks are labels
// built from gateTxt, so they are right about the CURRENT settings and silent
// about what they used to say.
//
// The eight marks themselves are drawn at the FOOT of this file, as labels. The
// history of that decision is worth keeping, because it was made twice:
//
// This block used to hold eight large plotshapes, four shapes for the four best
// lengths, each carrying its gate as const text - a literal like '<pct>/<min>' -
// because plotshape's text argument takes a const string and cannot be built from pctA
// and minA. That literal is a promise the file could not keep, so the live table
// grew a check that shouted MARKER TEXT IS STALE whenever a gate was edited away
// from its own label.
//
// The second lengths drew nothing at all, and the reason was a measured ceiling:
//
//   plotshape 2 - plotshape WITH TEXT 4 - bgcolor 1 - alertcondition 1, limit 64
//
// Text doubles a marker, so sixteen texted shapes are the whole budget before a
// single outcome marker is drawn - and before the seventeen alert conditions,
// which are plots too and were left out of this sum until they caused RE10140. The second length was the thing that went,
// because it is a CONTROL rather than a trade - it says whether the best length
// was a plateau or a spike.
//
// All of that is now moot. Labels cost no plot, take a series for their text,
// their size and their draw order, and there are eight glyphs where there were
// only six usable shapes. See THE EIGHT MARKS at the foot.

// NO WASH ANY MORE. The seven that are not leading used to be faded to 90 -
// nearly gone, but still there to be found if you looked. Now they are simply
// not drawn: the leading series has to be the thing the chart is ABOUT, and a
// chart with one gate open on a symbol is a chart that should show one marker,
// not eight at different degrees of see-through. It applies to the mark, to
// its caption and to its outcome alike, so a whole machine steps back together
// - see dimOff, folded straight into the eight entry and sixteen outcome flags
// rather than into their colour.
//
// THE COST OF THAT CHOICE: a signal that fired on a non-leading gate leaves no
// trace on the chart at all now, where it used to leave a pale one. It is still
// the same fact the closed log and the RUNNING table were already built
// around - only the LEADER ever had a row in either - so nothing that used to
// be readable stops being readable. What is gone is the chart-only evidence
// that something fired on a gate that was not carrying the episode, which is
// exactly the clutter this was asked to remove.

// THE ENTRY MARKS ARE DRAWN AT THE FOOT of this file - eight large shapes, one
// per machine, through location.absolute so that one plot call serves both
// sides. The outcome markers below are the small half of the same pair.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The outcome marker - small, and on the candle itself
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NO OFFSET, and that is the difference from everything above. A large marker is
// drawn one bar back onto the REJECTION candle, because that is the candle you
// enter after. This one reports what a candle DID, so it belongs on the candle
// that did it - the bar the script is standing on.
//
// So a finished entry reads as two marks a bar apart: the large diamond on the
// rejection candle, and if it lost, a small one on the candle that lost it.
// Nothing under the large marker means it paid, the window closed, and the
// ladder went back to the bottom.
//
// TWO MARKERS IN ONE SHAPE, and the reason is a hard ceiling rather than taste.
//
// THE PLOT BUDGET, measured and complete - the earlier version of this note was
// short by the largest term in it and cost a second RE10140:
//
//   plotshape                 2
//   plotshape WITH TEXT       4
//   bgcolor                   1
//   ALERTCONDITION            1     <- seventeen of them at the foot of this file
//   limit                     64
//
// An alert condition is invisible on the chart and is a plot all the same. This
// file carries seventeen, so a third of the budget is spent before a single
// shape is drawn, and twenty-four shapes came to 66. Both halves of the marker
// pair now use location.absolute - one call per machine instead of one per side
// - which is sixteen shapes. FOUR of them then split again, a call per side,
// because they POINT and plotshape's style is a const - the two pointing entry
// marks and the two pointing outcomes under them: twenty calls, 40 + 17 + 1 =
// 58, and six plots still spare.
//
// The merge costs nothing, because the two are colour-disjoint by construction:
//
//   the WHITE STEP is always white
//   a FAILURE is never white. streakColor returns white only at colorLimit - 1,
//     and landing there AFTER grading needs a pre-grading streak of
//     colorLimit - 2 - one short of the lowest streak that can produce a marker
//     at all. No failed marker can come out white.
//
// So the colour still says which of the two you are looking at. When both land
// on the same candle the failure wins, and nothing is lost there either: both
// need a signal, so the coincidence is a WARNING that failed, and a warning is
// already drawn large and white one bar back. The white step would be repeating
// what the large marker just said.
//
// Both halves keep their own tick box. Twelve more shapes on a chart that
// already carries twelve is a lot, and the point of the size is that they read
// as annotation rather than as instruction.
showFails = input.bool(true, 'Small marker when a large one fails', group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'The outcome of every large marker, in the colour of the rung the ladder reached with that candle counted. A large marker with nothing under it won - and a win closes the window, so the ladder stops there.', display = display.none)
showWhite = input.bool(true, 'Small white marker on the pre-armed step', group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'The moment a gate first reaches one step short of opening, which is the state the scanner paints a white row for. Without it the scanner can say pre-armed while the chart says nothing at all. It shares a shape with the failure marker above - white means the step, any other colour means a failure.', display = display.none)

// Softened rather than solid, exactly as maRejection.pine draws it. It marks a
// STATE, not an outcome and not a trade, so it should be legible without
// carrying the weight of a marker that reports something that happened.
WHITE_STEP = color.new(color.white, 25)

// One flag and one colour per slot and side, worked out here rather than inside
// the plotshape call. The colour has to agree with the flag about WHICH of the
// two fired - reading showFails in one and not the other is how a hidden failure
// would come out wearing its failure colour anyway.
// Washed with the large marker it belongs to, and by the same rule: an outcome
// that reports a marker standing behind the leader is itself standing behind it.
// Splitting the two would put a full-strength failure under a pale entry.
//
// AND IT WEARS THAT MARKER'S SHAPE, one size down - see THE EIGHT MARKS at the
// foot. Shape says which gate, size says entry-or-outcome, and the two questions
// stop competing for the same channel.
// ONE CALL PER MACHINE, both sides, exactly as the eight large marks below do
// it - and here it was not a nicety but the fix for RE10140. The plot ceiling is
// 64 and the accounting in this file was short by seventeen, because
// ALERTCONDITION COSTS A PLOT TOO. Seventeen alert conditions, one background,
// and twenty-four shapes at two apiece is 66. With both halves of the pair on
// location.absolute it is sixteen shapes, and twenty once the two pointing slots
// split a call per side at BOTH sizes - 40 + 17 + 1 = 58, and there is still
// room to breathe.
//
// Staggered by the same DENSE lane the large marks use, so a machine's outcome
// sits under its own mark rather than in a heap with the other seven - and only
// as far out as the bar actually needs. Tighter than the entry marks, because
// these are small and belong close to the candle that graded them.
//
// The lane is worked out AFTER all sixteen flags below, not inside this
// function, because a lane is a fact about the whole bar rather than about one
// slot: it has to count the other seven before it knows where this one goes.
smY(bool lng, bool sht, int ln) =>
    gap = atrVal * (0.20 + ln * 0.30)
    lng ? low - gap : sht ? high + gap : na

a1SmL = liveA and ((showFails and a1FailL) or (showWhite and a1WhL))
a1SmS = liveA and ((showFails and a1FailS) or (showWhite and a1WhS))
a1SmC = washCol(a1SmL ? (showFails and a1FailL ? a1FailC : WHITE_STEP) : (showFails and a1FailS ? a1FailC : WHITE_STEP), 0)

a2SmL = liveA and ((showFails and a2FailL) or (showWhite and a2WhL))
a2SmS = liveA and ((showFails and a2FailS) or (showWhite and a2WhS))
a2SmC = washCol(a2SmL ? (showFails and a2FailL ? a2FailC : WHITE_STEP) : (showFails and a2FailS ? a2FailC : WHITE_STEP), 1)

b1SmL = liveB and ((showFails and b1FailL) or (showWhite and b1WhL))
b1SmS = liveB and ((showFails and b1FailS) or (showWhite and b1WhS))
b1SmC = washCol(b1SmL ? (showFails and b1FailL ? b1FailC : WHITE_STEP) : (showFails and b1FailS ? b1FailC : WHITE_STEP), 2)

b2SmL = liveB and ((showFails and b2FailL) or (showWhite and b2WhL))
b2SmS = liveB and ((showFails and b2FailS) or (showWhite and b2WhS))
b2SmC = washCol(b2SmL ? (showFails and b2FailL ? b2FailC : WHITE_STEP) : (showFails and b2FailS ? b2FailC : WHITE_STEP), 3)

c1SmL = liveC and ((showFails and c1FailL) or (showWhite and c1WhL))
c1SmS = liveC and ((showFails and c1FailS) or (showWhite and c1WhS))
c1SmC = washCol(c1SmL ? (showFails and c1FailL ? c1FailC : WHITE_STEP) : (showFails and c1FailS ? c1FailC : WHITE_STEP), 4)

c2SmL = liveC and ((showFails and c2FailL) or (showWhite and c2WhL))
c2SmS = liveC and ((showFails and c2FailS) or (showWhite and c2WhS))
c2SmC = washCol(c2SmL ? (showFails and c2FailL ? c2FailC : WHITE_STEP) : (showFails and c2FailS ? c2FailC : WHITE_STEP), 5)

d1SmL = liveD and ((showFails and d1FailL) or (showWhite and d1WhL))
d1SmS = liveD and ((showFails and d1FailS) or (showWhite and d1WhS))
d1SmC = washCol(d1SmL ? (showFails and d1FailL ? d1FailC : WHITE_STEP) : (showFails and d1FailS ? d1FailC : WHITE_STEP), 6)

d2SmL = liveD and ((showFails and d2FailL) or (showWhite and d2WhL))
d2SmS = liveD and ((showFails and d2FailS) or (showWhite and d2WhS))
d2SmC = washCol(d2SmL ? (showFails and d2FailL ? d2FailC : WHITE_STEP) : (showFails and d2FailS ? d2FailC : WHITE_STEP), 7)

// THE DENSE LANES for the outcome marks, worked out from the sixteen flags
// above. A slot is "drawing" when either of its sides is, and a slot that draws
// nothing takes no lane at all - which is what stops one small diamond on gate D
// from standing where eight would have stood.
oF0 = a1SmL or a1SmS
oF1 = a2SmL or a2SmS
oF2 = b1SmL or b1SmS
oF3 = b2SmL or b2SmS
oF4 = c1SmL or c1SmS
oF5 = c2SmL or c2SmS
oF6 = d1SmL or d1SmS
oF7 = d2SmL or d2SmS

b1SmY = smY(b1SmL, b1SmS, lane(2, oF0, oF1, oF2, oF3, oF4, oF5, oF6, oF7))
b2SmY = smY(b2SmL, b2SmS, lane(3, oF0, oF1, oF2, oF3, oF4, oF5, oF6, oF7))
c1SmY = smY(c1SmL, c1SmS, lane(4, oF0, oF1, oF2, oF3, oF4, oF5, oF6, oF7))
c2SmY = smY(c2SmL, c2SmS, lane(5, oF0, oF1, oF2, oF3, oF4, oF5, oF6, oF7))
d1SmY = smY(d1SmL, d1SmS, lane(6, oF0, oF1, oF2, oF3, oF4, oF5, oF6, oF7))
d2SmY = smY(d2SmL, d2SmS, lane(7, oF0, oF1, oF2, oF3, oF4, oF5, oF6, oF7))

// The two POINTING slots split a call per side here exactly as their entry marks
// do at the foot, and for the same reason: plotshape's style is a const, so a
// shape that has to point with the trade needs one call for each direction. The
// lane is worked out once and handed to both, so the pair cannot drift apart.
a1SmYL = smY(a1SmL, false,  lane(0, oF0, oF1, oF2, oF3, oF4, oF5, oF6, oF7))
a1SmYS = smY(false,  a1SmS, lane(0, oF0, oF1, oF2, oF3, oF4, oF5, oF6, oF7))
a2SmYL = smY(a2SmL, false,  lane(1, oF0, oF1, oF2, oF3, oF4, oF5, oF6, oF7))
a2SmYS = smY(false,  a2SmS, lane(1, oF0, oF1, oF2, oF3, oF4, oF5, oF6, oF7))

plotshape(b1SmY, 'B ● outcome', shape.circle,  location.absolute, color = b1SmC, size = size.tiny)
plotshape(b2SmY, 'B ■ outcome', shape.square,  location.absolute, color = b2SmC, size = size.tiny)
plotshape(c1SmY, 'C ⚑ outcome', shape.flag,    location.absolute, color = c1SmC, size = size.tiny)
plotshape(c2SmY, 'C ◆ outcome', shape.diamond, location.absolute, color = c2SmC, size = size.tiny)
plotchar(d1SmY, title = 'D ☀ outcome', char = '☀', location = location.absolute, color = d1SmC, size = size.tiny)
plotchar(d2SmY, title = 'D ❄ outcome', char = '❄', location = location.absolute, color = d2SmC, size = size.tiny)
plotshape(a1SmYL, 'A ▲ outcome · long',  shape.triangleup,   location.absolute, color = a1SmC, size = size.tiny)
plotshape(a1SmYS, 'A ▼ outcome · short', shape.triangledown, location.absolute, color = a1SmC, size = size.tiny)
plotshape(a2SmYL, 'A ⬆ outcome · long',  shape.labelup,      location.absolute, color = a2SmC, size = size.tiny)
plotshape(a2SmYS, 'A ⬇ outcome · short', shape.labeldown,    location.absolute, color = a2SmC, size = size.tiny)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The two tables
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// mar1Scanner.pine's RUNNING table and its CLOSED log, column for column. A row
// there is a pair; a row here is one of the eight machines on this symbol. Both keep
// their summary line, which is the part that is read first and most often.
//
// Anchors follow the scanner's own screen: CLOSED on the left, RUNNING at the
// bottom. Two tables sharing an anchor overlap with nothing on screen to say so,
// which is why the positions are settings rather than guesses.
C_HEAD = #3B404A
C_ROWA = #1F242D
C_TXT  = #F2F6FA
C_DIM  = #7C8792
C_FOOT = #1A1E25

// The eight, gathered into arrays so the two tables can loop over them instead of
// naming every slot twice. Rebuilt on the last bar only - these are reads of
// state the machines already hold.
// THE EIGHT GLYPHS, and they are the shapes this file draws on the candles -
// not a parallel alphabet for the tables. A row that names a machine one way
// and a mark that names it another is one mapping too many to hold in the head.
//
// ▲ and ⬆ are directional and so is the Side column, so a short on either of
// those two reads with the glyph facing one way and the side the other. That
// cost was weighed and taken: same symbol on the chart and in the table.
slotGlyph(int s) => s == 0 ? '▲' : s == 1 ? '⬆' : s == 2 ? '●' : s == 3 ? '■' : s == 4 ? '⚑' : s == 5 ? '◆' : s == 6 ? '☀' : '❄'
slotLen(int s)   => s == 0 ? lenA1 : s == 1 ? lenA2 : s == 2 ? lenB1 : s == 3 ? lenB2 : s == 4 ? lenC1 : s == 5 ? lenC2 : s == 6 ? lenD1 : lenD2
slotTag(int s)   => slotGlyph(s) + ' ' + str.tostring(slotLen(s))

// What each gate is SET to, read live. The markers carry the same numbers as
// const text, and this is the only copy that cannot go stale - so the legend
// row and the drift warning below are both built from these.
gatePct(int s)   => s < 2 ? pctA : s < 4 ? pctB : s < 6 ? pctC : pctD
gateMin(int s)   => s < 2 ? minA : s < 4 ? minB : s < 6 ? minC : minD
gateTxt(int s)   => str.tostring(gatePct(s), '#.##') + '/' + str.tostring(gateMin(s))

runPos = input.string('Bottom right', 'RUNNING table position',
     options = ['Bottom right', 'Bottom center', 'Middle right', 'Top right'], group = gOut, display = display.none)
logPos = input.string('Middle left', 'CLOSED log position',
     options = ['Middle left', 'Top left', 'Bottom left', 'Middle center'], group = gOut, display = display.none)
showLog = input.bool(false, 'Show the closed log', group = gOut,
     tooltip = "Off by default, for the same reason as the table above. The log is still RECORDED whatever this says - ticking it on shows everything that closed inside the window, not just what closed since. For the middle setting - the summary line without the rows - leave this ticked and set 'Show the closed log as' to Summary only.", display = display.none)

// How tall each block of the closed log is. 0 is auto: twenty tall until all
// four blocks are in use, then taller - the same rule mar1Scanner.pine uses, and
// the same reason. Width is the direction a chart has to spare.
logPerCol = input.int(0, 'Closed log: rows per column (0 = auto)', minval = 0, maxval = 60, group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'The list wraps into blocks side by side rather than growing down. 0 sizes the blocks itself.', display = display.none)

LOG_BLOCKS = 4
LOG_TALL   = 60

// HOW MANY of those blocks the log may use, where rows per column decides how
// TALL each one is. Together they are the whole of what the table shows - rows
// times blocks - and the header prints the product, so the two never have to be
// multiplied in your head.
//
// Declared HERE rather than beside its partner four hundred lines up, and for
// two reasons that happen to agree. It needs LOG_BLOCKS for its own ceiling, and
// LOG_BLOCKS is the line above. And TradingView maps a saved setting onto an
// input by POSITION: every other input in this file is declared above this
// point, so appending here is the one place a new input costs nobody a reset.
// The dialog lays inputs out by their group, not by where they sit in the file,
// so this still appears under Tables beside the rows-per-column it belongs with.
logCols = input.int(0, 'Closed log: blocks side by side (0 = auto)', minval = 0, maxval = LOG_BLOCKS, group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'How many columns of the log stand beside each other. 0 uses all four. One block with a tall rows-per-column gives a single long list; two of thirty is the same sixty rows in half the width. Together with rows per column this is the whole of the layout - the header says which shape it ended up with whenever either is set by hand.', display = display.none)

posOf(string nm) => nm == 'Bottom center' ? position.bottom_center :
     nm == 'Middle right' ? position.middle_right :
     nm == 'Top right' ? position.top_right :
     nm == 'Top left' ? position.top_left :
     nm == 'Bottom left' ? position.bottom_left :
     nm == 'Middle center' ? position.middle_center :
     nm == 'Middle left' ? position.middle_left : position.bottom_right

var table runTable = table.new(posOf(runPos), 7, 14, border_width = 1)
var table logTable = table.new(posOf(logPos), LOG_BLOCKS * 6 - 1, LOG_TALL + 2, border_width = 1)

// Which row each slot landed on this pass, or -1 if it did not draw a row at
// all. Read below, after livePrev - the RUNNING table itself has to draw
// before livePrev exists (see WHY IT LIVES HERE at livePrev's own
// declaration), so this is how a later block finds the right row to repaint
// without moving the table's hundred and fifty lines down there with it.
var int[] runRow = array.new_int(8, -1)

if barstate.islast and showTable
    table.clear(runTable, 0, 0, 6, 13)
    array.fill(runRow, -1)

    // The three states, counted before anything is drawn, because the header has
    // to be able to say NOTHING OPEN - and it can only know that after every
    // slot has been asked.
    //
    //   RUNNING     entries taken, none has paid yet
    //   ARMED       the gate is open and no entry has happened on it
    //   pre-armed   one step short of the anchor, so the next marker is white
    //
    // pre-armed is not "nearly running". Nothing is open on a pre-armed gate and
    // none of it reaches the closed log; it is a gate worth watching, which is
    // why it is the faintest row in the table.
    int nRun = 0
    int nArm = 0
    int nPre = 0
    for s = 0 to 7
        if slotOn(s) and tfOk
            ent = s == 0 ? a1Win : s == 1 ? a2Win : s == 2 ? b1Win : s == 3 ? b2Win :
                 s == 4 ? c1Win : s == 5 ? c2Win : s == 6 ? d1Win : d2Win
            opn = s == 0 ? a1Open : s == 1 ? a2Open : s == 2 ? b1Open : s == 3 ? b2Open :
                 s == 4 ? c1Open : s == 5 ? c2Open : s == 6 ? d1Open : d2Open
            stk = s == 0 ? a1Streak : s == 1 ? a2Streak : s == 2 ? b1Streak : s == 3 ? b2Streak :
                 s == 4 ? c1Streak : s == 5 ? c2Streak : s == 6 ? d1Streak : d2Streak
            lim = s == 0 ? a1Lim : s == 1 ? a2Lim : s == 2 ? b1Lim : s == 3 ? b2Lim :
                 s == 4 ? c1Lim : s == 5 ? c2Lim : s == 6 ? d1Lim : d2Lim
            // effOpn / effPre - see the note beside their reuse further down,
            // at the row loop that draws the same eight slots. Applied here
            // too so the header count and the rows under it can never
            // disagree about whether something is RUNNING this exact tick.
            inMkt = s == 0 ? a1Buy or a1Sell : s == 1 ? a2Buy or a2Sell : s == 2 ? b1Buy or b1Sell : s == 3 ? b2Buy or b2Sell :
                 s == 4 ? c1Buy or c1Sell : s == 5 ? c2Buy or c2Sell : s == 6 ? d1Buy or d1Sell : d2Buy or d2Sell
            effOpn = s == 0 ? a1EffOpn : s == 1 ? a2EffOpn : s == 2 ? b1EffOpn : s == 3 ? b2EffOpn :
                 s == 4 ? c1EffOpn : s == 5 ? c2EffOpn : s == 6 ? d1EffOpn : d2EffOpn
            effPre = s == 0 ? a1EffPre : s == 1 ? a2EffPre : s == 2 ? b1EffPre : s == 3 ? b2EffPre :
                 s == 4 ? c1EffPre : s == 5 ? c2EffPre : s == 6 ? d1EffPre : d2EffPre
            if ent > 0 or inMkt
                nRun += 1
            else if opn or effOpn
                nArm += 1
            else if (lim > 0 and stk == lim - 1) or effPre
                nPre += 1

    // THE LEGEND, and it is the half of the marker text that cannot go stale.
    //
    // THE STALE-TEXT WARNING THAT USED TO LIVE HERE IS GONE, and its going is the
    // point. The chart used to paint each gate's number as a const literal, so a
    // gate edited away from that number left the markers lying and this header had to shout
    // about it. The marks are labels now and their text is built from gateTxt -
    // the same function this legend uses - so there is nothing left to disagree.
    string legend = ''
    for s = 0 to 7
        if s % 2 == 0
            legend := legend + (s > 0 ? '   ' : ' ') + slotGlyph(s) + ' ' + gateTxt(s) +
                 ' MA' + str.tostring(slotLen(s)) + '/' + str.tostring(slotLen(s + 1))

    hdBg = not tfOk ? #553300 : nRun > 0 ? #4A3B14 : #0E2A1B
    table.cell(runTable, 0, 0,
         not tfOk ? ' MAR1 GATES  ·  ' + timeframe.period + ' IS NOT ' + TF_LIVE + ' ' :
          nRun + nArm + nPre == 0 ? ' MAR1 GATES  ·  NOTHING OPEN ' :
          ' MAR1 GATES  ·  ' + str.tostring(nRun) + ' RUNNING   ' + str.tostring(nArm) + ' ARMED ' +
          (nPre > 0 ? '  ·  ' + str.tostring(nPre) + ' pre-armed ' : ''),
         bgcolor = hdBg, text_color = not tfOk ? #FFC163 : nRun > 0 ? #FFD98A : #6EF0A5, text_size = txtSize)
    for k = 1 to 6
        table.cell(runTable, k, 0, '', bgcolor = hdBg, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(runTable, 0, 0, 6, 0)

    // The legend gets its own row under the header, because the four gates are
    // now four shapes and nobody remembers which cross is which without it.
    table.cell(runTable, 0, 1, legend + ' ', bgcolor = C_FOOT, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
         tooltip = 'Every gate, its percentage over its minimum entries, and the two MA lengths it is running on this timeframe - read from the settings, not from the labels on the chart.')
    for k = 1 to 6
        table.cell(runTable, k, 1, '', bgcolor = C_FOOT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(runTable, 0, 1, 6, 1)

    table.cell(runTable, 0, 2, ' Gate ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
         tooltip = 'The glyph is the SAME shape this machine wears on the chart and the number is the length it is running on this timeframe. ▲ ⬆ gate A, ● ■ gate B, ⚑ ◆ gate C, ☀ ❄ gate D - the first of each pair is the best length, the second the control. ◀ marks the LEADING series: whoever was there FIRST and has not finished. An open window outranks a pre-armed gate whatever the clock says, and inside a tier the earliest wins. Only the leader draws on the candles at all; every other slot draws nothing there until its own turn comes. A row here still exists for every gate that is running, armed or pre-armed, whether it is leading or not - a mark on the chart is a series with a row in the closed log; a series that never led while it ran has none.')
    table.cell(runTable, 1, 2, ' State ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
         tooltip = 'RUNNING - entries taken, none has paid yet. ARMED - the gate is open, no entry yet. pre-armed - one step short of the anchor, so the next marker would come out white. Nothing is open on a pre-armed gate and none of it reaches the closed log.')
    table.cell(runTable, 2, 2, ' Market ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
         tooltip = 'Whether a position is live on the candle now forming. An entry grades exactly one candle - taken at its open, settled at its close - so IN lasts one candle, while RUNNING can stand for an hour across candles you were flat for.')
    table.cell(runTable, 3, 2, ' Entries ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
         tooltip = 'What the open window has cost so far. This is the number a recovery ladder dies on.')
    table.cell(runTable, 4, 2, ' Open since ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
         tooltip = 'When the gate opened - the green diamond, or the first entry when the streak gate is Off. UTC+1.')
    table.cell(runTable, 5, 2, ' Streak / gate ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
         tooltip = 'Consecutive failures against the threshold that opens the gate.')
    table.cell(runTable, 6, 2, ' Last ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
         tooltip = 'The last signal this gate produced, and which way. Blank when it has not produced one yet.')

    int r = 3
    for s = 0 to 7
        ent = s == 0 ? a1Win : s == 1 ? a2Win : s == 2 ? b1Win : s == 3 ? b2Win :
             s == 4 ? c1Win : s == 5 ? c2Win : s == 6 ? d1Win : d2Win
        opn = s == 0 ? a1Open : s == 1 ? a2Open : s == 2 ? b1Open : s == 3 ? b2Open :
             s == 4 ? c1Open : s == 5 ? c2Open : s == 6 ? d1Open : d2Open
        stk = s == 0 ? a1Streak : s == 1 ? a2Streak : s == 2 ? b1Streak : s == 3 ? b2Streak :
             s == 4 ? c1Streak : s == 5 ? c2Streak : s == 6 ? d1Streak : d2Streak
        thr = s == 0 ? a1Thr : s == 1 ? a2Thr : s == 2 ? b1Thr : s == 3 ? b2Thr :
             s == 4 ? c1Thr : s == 5 ? c2Thr : s == 6 ? d1Thr : d2Thr
        lim = s == 0 ? a1Lim : s == 1 ? a2Lim : s == 2 ? b1Lim : s == 3 ? b2Lim :
             s == 4 ? c1Lim : s == 5 ? c2Lim : s == 6 ? d1Lim : d2Lim
        opT = s == 0 ? a1OpenT : s == 1 ? a2OpenT : s == 2 ? b1OpenT : s == 3 ? b2OpenT :
             s == 4 ? c1OpenT : s == 5 ? c2OpenT : s == 6 ? d1OpenT : d2OpenT
        lsT = s == 0 ? a1LastT : s == 1 ? a2LastT : s == 2 ? b1LastT : s == 3 ? b2LastT :
             s == 4 ? c1LastT : s == 5 ? c2LastT : s == 6 ? d1LastT : d2LastT
        lsD = s == 0 ? a1LastD : s == 1 ? a2LastD : s == 2 ? b1LastD : s == 3 ? b2LastD :
             s == 4 ? c1LastD : s == 5 ? c2LastD : s == 6 ? d1LastD : d2LastD
        inMkt = s == 0 ? a1Buy or a1Sell : s == 1 ? a2Buy or a2Sell : s == 2 ? b1Buy or b1Sell : s == 3 ? b2Buy or b2Sell :
             s == 4 ? c1Buy or c1Sell : s == 5 ? c2Buy or c2Sell : s == 6 ? d1Buy or d1Sell : d2Buy or d2Sell

        pre  = lim > 0 and stk == lim - 1
        live = slotOn(s) and tfOk

        // effOpn / effOpnT / effPre - the SAME one-tick-lag fix THE LEADING
        // SERIES already applies for ranking (a1EffOpn and its seven
        // siblings), reused here rather than reinvented. opn / ent / pre only
        // update inside gateOne's own barstate.isconfirmed block, so an entry
        // or warning that fires on the still-forming candle is already true
        // in inMkt (built from the same unconditional aXBuy / aXSell this
        // reuses) while opn / pre have not caught up for one more tick - and
        // without this fix, that meant NO ROW AT ALL for the exact tick a
        // star preview, or even the settled entry mark, appeared on the
        // chart. User report, 2026-08-11: the Market column read blank right
        // up to IN, with the ARMED / entry transition almost never visible in
        // between - because there was nothing here for THE BIG MARKET
        // INDICATOR below to repaint into.
        effOpn = s == 0 ? a1EffOpn : s == 1 ? a2EffOpn : s == 2 ? b1EffOpn : s == 3 ? b2EffOpn :
             s == 4 ? c1EffOpn : s == 5 ? c2EffOpn : s == 6 ? d1EffOpn : d2EffOpn
        effOpnT = s == 0 ? a1EffOpnT : s == 1 ? a2EffOpnT : s == 2 ? b1EffOpnT : s == 3 ? b2EffOpnT :
             s == 4 ? c1EffOpnT : s == 5 ? c2EffOpnT : s == 6 ? d1EffOpnT : d2EffOpnT
        effPre = s == 0 ? a1EffPre : s == 1 ? a2EffPre : s == 2 ? b1EffPre : s == 3 ? b2EffPre :
             s == 4 ? c1EffPre : s == 5 ? c2EffPre : s == 6 ? d1EffPre : d2EffPre

        // Idle gates are left out entirely. Eight rows of "idle" is eight rows of
        // nothing, and the whole point of this table is that what is on it is
        // what is happening.
        //
        // effOpn already implies opn (it is opn or aXBuy or aXSell) and effPre
        // already implies pre, so this is strictly a SUPERSET of the old
        // `ent > 0 or opn or pre` - every row that used to appear still does,
        // and the only change is rows that could not appear before.
        if live and (ent > 0 or effOpn or effPre) and r < 11
            // inMkt is reused rather than re-derived, so this row's idea of
            // "an entry just happened" can never disagree with the Market
            // cell painted for it two lines below.
            st = ent > 0 or inMkt ? 'RUNNING' : opn or effOpn ? 'ARMED' : 'pre-armed'
            // THE ROW IS THE LADDER, the same rule mar1GatesScanner.pine and
            // mar1Scanner.pine draw by: the background is the rung this window
            // stands on, so a purple row is three entries past its anchor and
            // says so before a number is read.
            //
            // A pre-armed row is white at 88 - a wash rather than a fill. It is
            // not a position: nothing is open, nothing has cost anything, and it
            // reaches no log. White rather than a fourth colour of its own,
            // because white is already what the ladder calls one step short of
            // the gate, and the marker this row reports is literally a white one.
            //
            // rungText is not consulted on a pre-armed row: it returns black for
            // the light rungs, and a wash at 88 is not a light background.
            //
            // opn / effOpn treated alike here - a fresh entry with entriesInWindow
            // still reading 0 gets the same rung an armed-but-unentered gate
            // already got, which is the honest reading: one entry either just
            // happened or is about to be counted, and either way this is not
            // the pale pre-armed step any more.
            rung = ent > 0 ? ladderRung(ent) : (opn or effOpn) ? 2 : 1
            bg = pre and not opn and not effOpn ? color.new(color.white, 88) : rungFill(rung, 55)
            fg = pre and not opn and not effOpn ? color.new(color.white, 30) : rungText(rung)
            // ◀ on the leading series, which is the row whose markers keep their
            // full colour on the chart. It earns its place here rather than only
            // out there because the leader can be a SECOND length, and those draw
            // no marker: without this the chart would go entirely pale with
            // nothing on it saying on whose behalf.
            table.cell(runTable, 0, r, ' ' + slotTag(s) + (s == leadS ? ' ◀' : '') + ' ', bgcolor = bg, text_color = fg, text_size = txtSizeBig)
            table.cell(runTable, 1, r, ' ' + st + ' ', bgcolor = bg, text_color = fg, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(runTable, 2, r, inMkt ? ' IN ' : (ent > 0 or opn or effOpn or effPre ? ' wait ' : ' '), bgcolor = bg,
                 text_color = inMkt ? color.white : fg, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(runTable, 3, r, ent > 0 ? ' ' + str.tostring(ent) + ' ' : ' ', bgcolor = bg,
                 text_color = fg, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(runTable, 4, r, (ent > 0 or opn or effOpn) and (opT > 0 or effOpnT > 0) ?
                 ' ' + str.format_time(opT > 0 ? opT : effOpnT, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1') + ' ' : ' ',
                 bgcolor = bg, text_color = fg, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(runTable, 5, r, ' ' + str.tostring(stk) + ' / ' + str.tostring(thr) + ' ', bgcolor = bg, text_color = fg, text_size = txtSize)
            // Same hue, darkened on a light rung and paled on the top one:
            // lime on lime and red on red are both invisible, and the row is
            // already carrying the alarm on the step where that matters. Only
            // rung 1 (pure white) needs it now - the gradient runs on 700-900
            // shades (2026-08-10), dark enough for the default arrow colours
            // to read fine, the same as 6 and 8 already did.
            light = not (pre and not opn and not effOpn) and rung == 1
            hot   = not (pre and not opn and not effOpn) and (rung == 6 or rung == 8)
            table.cell(runTable, 6, r, lsT > 0 ? (lsD == 1 ? ' ▲ long ' : ' ▼ short ') : ' ', bgcolor = bg,
                 text_color = lsT == 0 ? fg :
                      lsD == 1 ? (light ? color.new(#006622, 0) : hot ? #B9F6CA : color.lime) :
                      (light ? color.new(#991111, 0) : hot ? #FFFFFF : color.red), text_size = txtSize)
            array.set(runRow, s, r)
            r += 1

    if r == 3
        table.cell(runTable, 0, 3, tfOk ? ' nothing open on any of the eight ' : ' the grid has no lengths for this timeframe ',
             bgcolor = C_ROWA, text_color = C_DIM, text_size = txtSize)
        r += 1

    // The footer, and mar1Scanner.pine's reason for having one applies here
    // unchanged: with nothing open this table is one line saying so, which is
    // indistinguishable from a script that was never added, never resolved its
    // lengths, or quietly died. A clock that moves on every tick and a count of
    // what is actually live turns that into a positive statement - it is
    // working, and the answer is nothing.
    liveSlots = liveGates
    footBg = not tfOk ? color.new(color.red, 60) : color.new(#333333, 60)
    table.cell(runTable, 0, r,
         ' ' + str.tostring(tfOk ? liveSlots : 0) + ' of 8 gates live' +
         (liveSlots < 8 and tfOk ? '  ·  ' + str.tostring(8 - liveSlots) + ' unticked' : '') +
         (not tfOk ? '  ·  ' + timeframe.period + ' IS NOT ' + TF_LIVE + '' : '') +
         '  ·  ' + str.format_time(timenow, 'HH:mm:ss', 'UTC+1') + ' ',
         bgcolor = footBg, text_color = tfOk ? color.silver : color.white, text_size = txtSize)
    for k = 1 to 6
        table.cell(runTable, k, r, '', bgcolor = footBg, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(runTable, 0, r, 6, r)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Alerts
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One per slot per direction, because a slot is a trade: the gate says how the
// threshold was built and the MA length says which chart it was built on, and an
// alert that named neither would leave you guessing which of eight fired.
//
// Entries only. A warning is a heads-up that the gate is one step from opening,
// and it is on the chart as a white marker - it is not something to act on, so
// it does not wake anybody up.
//
// alertcondition messages are static, which is why the direction is in the
// condition rather than in the text.
alertcondition(a1Buy and liveA, 'Gate A best · long', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate A (55/4) best length, entry long')
alertcondition(a1Sell and liveA, 'Gate A best · short', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate A (55/4) best length, entry short')
alertcondition(a2Buy and liveA, 'Gate A second · long', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate A (55/4) second length, entry long')
alertcondition(a2Sell and liveA, 'Gate A second · short', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate A (55/4) second length, entry short')

alertcondition(b1Buy and liveB, 'Gate B best · long', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate B (80/5) best length, entry long')
alertcondition(b1Sell and liveB, 'Gate B best · short', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate B (80/5) best length, entry short')
alertcondition(b2Buy and liveB, 'Gate B second · long', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate B (80/5) second length, entry long')
alertcondition(b2Sell and liveB, 'Gate B second · short', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate B (80/5) second length, entry short')

alertcondition(c1Buy and liveC, 'Gate C best · long', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate C (90/6) best length, entry long')
alertcondition(c1Sell and liveC, 'Gate C best · short', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate C (90/6) best length, entry short')
alertcondition(c2Buy and liveC, 'Gate C second · long', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate C (90/6) second length, entry long')
alertcondition(c2Sell and liveC, 'Gate C second · short', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate C (90/6) second length, entry short')

alertcondition(d1Buy and liveD, 'Gate D best · long', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate D (95/7) best length, entry long')
alertcondition(d1Sell and liveD, 'Gate D best · short', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate D (95/7) best length, entry short')
alertcondition(d2Buy and liveD, 'Gate D second · long', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate D (95/7) second length, entry long')
alertcondition(d2Sell and liveD, 'Gate D second · short', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: gate D (95/7) second length, entry short')

// Any of the eight, for one alert that just says "look at the chart".
anyEntry = (liveA and (a1Buy or a1Sell or a2Buy or a2Sell)) or
     (liveB and (b1Buy or b1Sell or b2Buy or b2Sell)) or
     (liveC and (c1Buy or c1Sell or c2Buy or c2Sell)) or
     (liveD and (d1Buy or d1Sell or d2Buy or d2Sell))
alertcondition(anyEntry, 'Any gate · any entry', 'MAR1 Gates {{ticker}} {{interval}}: one of the eight took an entry')

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The indicator hours, drawn
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// maRejection.pine's trading-hours background, brought over and given edges.
//
// The hours are already a FILTER - outside them hoursOk is false and not one of
// the eight machines fires - but until now the only place that showed was a line
// of text on a table header. A chart with no band on it looks the same at 03:00
// as at 10:00, and a stretch of candles with no markers reads as "nothing
// triggered" when the truth is "nothing was allowed to".
//
// THE EDGES ARE THE POINT. A flat wash at 97 transparency, which is what
// maRejection.pine draws, is honest but almost invisible, and the request here
// was for something that lands in the eye. So the first bar inside the window
// and the first bar outside it are drawn at half the transparency of the body:
// two bright columns at the boundaries, a faint tint between them. The boundary
// is the thing you look for - the body only has to say which side you are on.
//
// DECLARED HERE, at the very foot of the file, and that is not laziness.
// TradingView maps a saved setting onto an input by POSITION, and every other
// input in this file is above this line: appending is the one place a new input
// costs nobody a reset on a chart already carrying the script. The dialog lays
// inputs out by their GROUP rather than by where they sit in the file, so these
// still appear under Tables with the rest of the display settings.
showHours = input.bool(true, 'Shade the indicator hours', group = gOut,
     tooltip = "The stretch of the day the hours filter allows signals in, as a tint with a bright column at each boundary. With 'Fire signals only inside those hours' switched off nothing is actually being restricted and the band is a marking only - the tint is drawn either way, because where the hours WOULD bite is still worth seeing.", display = display.none)
hoursTransp = input.int(90, 'How faint that shading is (100 = invisible)', minval = 70, maxval = 100, group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'Transparency of the body of the band. The two boundary columns are drawn at half of whatever is set here, so they stay legible however faint the middle is.', display = display.none)

// A boundary bar is the first one on its side of the line. Both are tested
// against the PREVIOUS bar rather than against a clock, so a session gap, a
// weekend or a missing candle moves the column with the data instead of leaving
// it standing on a bar that does not exist.
hoursOpens  = inHours and not inHours[1]
hoursCloses = not inHours and inHours[1]

// Half the transparency. The int() is not decoration: Pine's / returns a FLOAT
// even between two ints, and color.new wants an int transparency - 45.0 is a
// type error where 45 is a shade.
hoursEdgeTr = int(hoursTransp / 2)

// Built as a colour first and switched off second, so na is never the FIRST arm
// of the ternary - Pine infers the type of a conditional from the arm it reads
// first, and an na there gives it nothing to infer from.
hoursBg = hoursOpens ? color.new(#2E7D32, hoursEdgeTr) :
     hoursCloses ? color.new(#8E2F2F, hoursEdgeTr) :
     inHours ? color.new(#2E7D32, hoursTransp) :
     color.new(color.white, 100)

bgcolor(showHours ? hoursBg : color.new(color.white, 100), title = 'Indicator hours')

// THE FLAT, shaded - see THE FLAT, shaded - optional, and moved here, at the
// foot of the file, for what draws it and why it moved.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE EIGHT MARKS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The same reading maRejection.pine gives, extended to eight machines: a shape
// on the rejection candle - the candle you enter after - and a smaller one on
// the candle that graded it. The big one means a decision, the small one means
// an outcome, and the two must never be mistaken for one another. They were,
// when the marks were glyph text: a signal and a confirmation-of-failure at
// similar weight is a chart that has to be squinted at.
//
// size.normal against size.tiny, and the reason that spread is safe NOW when it
// was not before is that the outcome no longer has to carry identity by itself.
//
// It used to. Eight machines all put a size.small DIAMOND under their marks, so
// the only way to learn which gate an outcome belonged to was to trace the lane
// up to the entry above it - and with a wash on seven of the eight, a tiny
// diamond was a dot that said nothing. That is why tiny had been rejected once:
// a shape carrying the whole answer cannot be shrunk to where it is unreadable.
//
// AN OUTCOME NOW WEARS ITS OWN GATE'S SHAPE, minimised. The small mark under a
// flag is a flag; under an arrow, an arrow pointing the same way. So the shape
// answers "whose?" on sight, the lane no longer has to be counted, and the size
// is free to drop to where it belongs - annotation, not instruction.
//
// THE TWO THIN SHAPES NO LONGER NEED A SIZE OF THEIR OWN. Cross and xcross used
// to draw one size up here and at the entry marks - a stroke outline and a
// filled body at the same nominal size are not the same weight on a chart, and
// a tiny cross read as a scratch rather than a mark. Gate D now draws through
// plotchar instead of plotshape, and a solid character - ☀, ❄ - carries its own
// weight at the same size.normal / size.tiny every other machine already uses,
// so the exception is gone rather than moved.
//
// Three things still separate an entry from an outcome, and only one of them is
// size: an entry is on the PREVIOUS bar and has a caption; an outcome is on the
// bar itself and is never captioned.
//
// EIGHT SHAPES, ONE PER MACHINE, and the caption spells out which:
//
//   ▲▼ triangle A best      ⚑ flag       C best
//   ⬆⬇ arrow    A second    ◆ diamond    C second
//   ● circle    B best      ☀ sun        D best
//   ■ square    B second    ❄ snowflake  D second
//
// This was cut down to four for a while and the two reasons were not equally
// good. Both are answered rather than avoided now:
//
//   THE TRIANGLES LIED, and that one was real. A triangle pointing UP drawn
//   ABOVE the candle - which is where this chart puts a short - is a second
//   thing claiming to mean direction and claiming the opposite of the truth.
//
//   THE FIX IS TO MAKE THEM TELL THE TRUTH rather than to remove them. A shape
//   that points now points WITH the trade: below the candle, which is a long,
//   the tip is up; above the candle, which is a short, the tip is down. Position
//   and tip say the same thing, so the tip is a second, redundant, INSTANT
//   reading of the side rather than a contradiction.
//
//   That costs one extra plot call per pointing slot, because plotshape's style
//   is a const and cannot be chosen at runtime. Two slots point, so it is two
//   calls, and the budget below has room for them.
//
//   Slots 0 and 1 must point in DIFFERENT FAMILIES - a triangle and an arrow -
//   because the thing that used to tell them apart was that one always pointed
//   up and the other always pointed down. That was exactly the lie. With both
//   now following the trade, the family is the only thing left to separate them.
//
//   THE CROSSES VANISHED, and that one was a sizing problem dressed up as a
//   design principle. shape.cross and shape.xcross are thin strokes and beside a
//   filled diamond at the same nominal size they read as a scratch. They came
//   back drawn ONE SIZE UP - size.large against size.normal - which put them
//   back on equal footing. A stroke needs more nominal size than a fill to carry
//   the same weight; that was a fact about the shapes, not a reason to drop them.
//
//   AND THEN THEY LEFT AGAIN, this time for good rather than for size.
//   shape.cross and shape.xcross are gone from this file - gate D draws through
//   plotchar now, with ☀ and ❄ standing in for them. A solid character does not
//   need the size-up a stroke did, so gate D draws at the same size.normal and
//   size.tiny as the other six, and the exception above is history rather than
//   a rule still in force.
//
// SHAPE IS GLUED TO THE GATE - triangle, circle, flag, sun for the four best
// lengths, in gate order - which is the alphabet the settings dialog, the RUNNING
// table and the scanner all already use. The second lengths carry the other four.
//
// BOTH POINTING SHAPES SIT ON GATE A, and that is a deliberate reversal from an
// even earlier layout. They used to be split across two other gates so that no
// gate carried two directional glyphs at once. Gate A is currently the loosest
// gate in the set of four, the one that opens on the shortest streak and
// therefore fires most often - and it was asked to be the one the eye finds
// first. A triangle and an arrow are the two loudest shapes available, so they
// went there together. That placement is on the LETTER, not on the gate's
// current numbers - see GLYPH IS GLUED TO THE LETTER above - so it survives the
// next reshuffle too.
//
// THE TABLES CARRY THESE SAME EIGHT, and that was argued the other way first.
// The seconds used to be hollow versions of their gate's glyph - ◇ ✛ ○ □ - on
// the grounds that a table row has a Side column right beside its Gate column,
// so a pointing glyph there repeats the contradiction the triangles were guilty
// of on the chart.
//
// The trade went the other way, deliberately. Hollow glyphs mean the chart and
// the tables name the same machine differently, which is a mapping to be held in
// the head every time a row is matched to a mark - and matching rows to marks is
// the whole reason both exist. One alphabet, used everywhere, is worth more than
// avoiding a collision that only appears on two of the eight machines and only
// on shorts. The glyph is always first in a cell and the arrow always second, so
// position tells them apart even when they disagree.
//
// location.absolute IS WHAT MAKES EIGHT SHAPES FIT. plotshape charges two plots
// a call, an alertcondition charges one, and this file has seventeen of those -
// so the real room for shapes is 46 of the 64, which is twenty-three calls. Two
// sides times two halves of the marker pair times eight machines is thirty-two,
// and that is RE10140.
//
// With an absolute location the plotted VALUE is the price, so ONE call serves
// both sides: na on the bars where that machine said nothing, a price under the
// candle for a long and over it for a short. Sixteen calls instead of
// thirty-two - twenty with the two pointing slots split at both sizes - 40 plots
// of the 46, and room left over.
//
// STACKED, NOT PILED, is why the marks were separated in PRICE rather than left
// to z-order: plots are drawn in the order they are DECLARED - fixed when the
// file compiles - so a white warning on gate D used to cover a lime entry on
// gate A for no better reason than that D is written lower down, and nothing
// can reorder that at runtime. That machinery - every machine at its own
// distance from the candle, in ATR - is still here, but only the LEADER ever
// draws now, so it is also the only one that ever reaches the front row.
//
// THE LEADER IS THE ONLY ONE THAT DRAWS. The other seven are not washed, they
// are simply absent, and the leader holds its place from the moment it opened,
// or from the moment it reached the white step, until it finishes. Nothing
// appears after it while it is running.
showSecond = input.bool(true, 'Mark the gates on the chart', group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'One machine at a time, on the candle you enter after: whichever gate opened its window first (or, if none has, whichever reached the white step first) is the only one that draws. The two that point - the triangle and the arrow - point WITH the trade: tip up below the candle for a long, tip down above it for a short. Untick to clear the candles entirely; the tables keep working.', display = display.none)

// Where a mark sits, in price. The front row at 0.35 ATR from the candle and
// every other machine that is DRAWING a further 0.50 behind it - see lane, which
// counts what is on the bar rather than which slot it came from.
//
// THE NUMBERS HAVE COME DOWN TWICE and the second time was not about the step.
// 0.55 and 0.95 over eight reserved lanes put the outermost mark eight ATR up,
// which is far enough that a mark stops pointing at its own candle; dense lanes
// and 0.40 / 0.60 fixed most of that. What was left was subtler and shows on a
// chart as one mark hugging the candle while the next two float:
//
//   THE LEADER'S LANE WAS RESERVED ON EVERY BAR, drawn or not - see lane. The
//   leader is usually a slot that opened a window some candles back and is
//   silent now, so the front row stood empty and everything else queued behind
//   an absence. That is a whole lane of daylight for nobody, on most bars.
//
//   THE CAPTION REACHED THE NEXT LANE. It was drawn 0.55 out from its own mark
//   while the lanes were 0.60 apart, so the gate text for lane n landed 0.05 ATR
//   short of the MARK on lane n + 1 - which is a caption sitting on somebody
//   else's shape, and is what the white square under the next lane's own
//   caption was. It is 0.20 now: closer to its own mark than to the next one,
//   by construction.
//
// So a bar with two marks spends 0.35 and 0.85, and the caption never crosses
// the midpoint between them.
//
// low[1] and high[1], not low and high: offset = -1 lands the mark on the
// PREVIOUS candle, and a gap measured off the wrong bar is right on a quiet one
// and wrong beside a long one.
markY(bool lng, bool sht, int ln) =>
    gap = atrVal * (0.35 + ln * 0.50)
    showSecond ? (lng ? low[1] - gap : sht ? high[1] + gap : na) : na

// GENERATED - one call per machine, both sides, EXCEPT the two that point.
//
// plotshape's style is a CONST, so a shape cannot choose its own direction at
// runtime. The two pointing slots are therefore split into a long call and a
// short call, and each side is fed by passing false for the other - markY
// returns na for a side it was not given, and na draws nothing.
// The side flags first, then the DENSE LANES they imply, then the prices. Three
// steps rather than one, because a lane cannot be worked out from one slot: it
// counts the other seven.
eL0 = liveA and (a1Buy or a1BuyW)
eS0 = liveA and (a1Sell or a1SellW)
eL1 = liveA and (a2Buy or a2BuyW)
eS1 = liveA and (a2Sell or a2SellW)
eL2 = liveB and (b1Buy or b1BuyW)
eS2 = liveB and (b1Sell or b1SellW)
eL3 = liveB and (b2Buy or b2BuyW)
eS3 = liveB and (b2Sell or b2SellW)
eL4 = liveC and (c1Buy or c1BuyW)
eS4 = liveC and (c1Sell or c1SellW)
eL5 = liveC and (c2Buy or c2BuyW)
eS5 = liveC and (c2Sell or c2SellW)
eL6 = liveD and (d1Buy or d1BuyW)
eS6 = liveD and (d1Sell or d1SellW)
eL7 = liveD and (d2Buy or d2BuyW)
eS7 = liveD and (d2Sell or d2SellW)

eF0 = eL0 or eS0
eF1 = eL1 or eS1
eF2 = eL2 or eS2
eF3 = eL3 or eS3
eF4 = eL4 or eS4
eF5 = eL5 or eS5
eF6 = eL6 or eS6
eF7 = eL7 or eS7

eN0 = lane(0, eF0, eF1, eF2, eF3, eF4, eF5, eF6, eF7)
eN1 = lane(1, eF0, eF1, eF2, eF3, eF4, eF5, eF6, eF7)
eN2 = lane(2, eF0, eF1, eF2, eF3, eF4, eF5, eF6, eF7)
eN3 = lane(3, eF0, eF1, eF2, eF3, eF4, eF5, eF6, eF7)
eN4 = lane(4, eF0, eF1, eF2, eF3, eF4, eF5, eF6, eF7)
eN5 = lane(5, eF0, eF1, eF2, eF3, eF4, eF5, eF6, eF7)
eN6 = lane(6, eF0, eF1, eF2, eF3, eF4, eF5, eF6, eF7)
eN7 = lane(7, eF0, eF1, eF2, eF3, eF4, eF5, eF6, eF7)

b1Y = markY(eL2, eS2, eN2)
b2Y = markY(eL3, eS3, eN3)
c1Y = markY(eL4, eS4, eN4)
c2Y = markY(eL5, eS5, eN5)
d1Y = markY(eL6, eS6, eN6)
d2Y = markY(eL7, eS7, eN7)

a1YL = markY(eL0, false, eN0)
a1YS = markY(false, eS0, eN0)
a2YL = markY(eL1, false, eN1)
a2YS = markY(false, eS1, eN1)

plotshape(b1Y, 'B ● best',   shape.circle,       location.absolute, color = washCol(b1Col, 2), size = size.normal, offset = -1)
plotshape(b2Y, 'B ■ second', shape.square,       location.absolute, color = washCol(b2Col, 3), size = size.normal, offset = -1)
plotshape(c1Y, 'C ⚑ best',   shape.flag,         location.absolute, color = washCol(c1Col, 4), size = size.normal, offset = -1)
plotshape(c2Y, 'C ◆ second', shape.diamond,      location.absolute, color = washCol(c2Col, 5), size = size.normal, offset = -1)
plotchar(d1Y, title = 'D ☀ best',   char = '☀', location = location.absolute, color = washCol(d1Col, 6), size = size.normal, offset = -1)
plotchar(d2Y, title = 'D ❄ second', char = '❄', location = location.absolute, color = washCol(d2Col, 7), size = size.normal, offset = -1)

// THE TWO THAT POINT, and they point WITH the trade in both directions. Below
// the candle is a long and the tip is up; above the candle is a short and the
// tip is down. There is no arrangement in which a tip disagrees with the side
// its own position already states.
plotshape(a1YL, 'A ▲ best · long',    shape.triangleup,   location.absolute, color = washCol(a1Col, 0), size = size.normal, offset = -1)
plotshape(a1YS, 'A ▼ best · short',   shape.triangledown, location.absolute, color = washCol(a1Col, 0), size = size.normal, offset = -1)
plotshape(a2YL, 'A ⬆ second · long',  shape.labelup,      location.absolute, color = washCol(a2Col, 1), size = size.normal, offset = -1)
plotshape(a2YS, 'A ⬇ second · short', shape.labeldown,    location.absolute, color = washCol(a2Col, 1), size = size.normal, offset = -1)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The gate under the mark
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A LABEL, because plotshape's text is a const string and this one is not: it is
// read live from the inputs, so a gate edited to 97 says 97 rather than going on
// claiming the number it shipped with. That literal is why this file once needed
// a table that shouted MARKER TEXT IS STALE; there is nothing left to shout.
//
// size.small, and in the same colour as the mark it belongs to - a caption that
// competes with what it captions is the fault this whole rework started from, but
// size.tiny had gone past quiet into unreadable, which is a different fault. The
// gate is the one thing on the mark that cannot be guessed from the shape, so it
// has to survive being looked at. Small against a LARGE shape is still plainly
// subordinate. It never needs a wash of its own: gateLbl only ever draws for the
// leader, because y is na for everything else, so there is nothing to fade.
//
// 0.20 ATR OUT, and that number is tied to the lane step rather than chosen. The
// lanes are 0.50 apart, so anything past 0.25 puts the caption nearer the NEXT
// machine's mark than its own - which is not a crowded chart, it is a caption
// that says the wrong thing. It was 0.55 against a 0.60 step, five hundredths of
// an ATR from landing on the shape above it, and on a busy bar it landed.
//
// Only 500 labels exist and the oldest are dropped, so the captions cover the
// recent history and the SHAPES cover all of it. That is the right way round -
// the shape is what identifies the machine, and the caption only spells out what
// the shape already says.
//
// openT IS WHEN THIS WINDOW ACTUALLY OPENED, not when this mark was drawn - and
// the two differ exactly in the case a caption needs to explain. A slot that
// spent its whole open life hidden behind another leader draws its first mark
// on the bar it TAKES the lead, and without a since-when on that mark it reads
// as a series that just started, when it may have been running for an hour
// underneath the one that was showing. aXSigT is passed rather than aXOpenT for
// the same reason it exists at all: on the bar a window WINS, aXOpenT has
// already been reset to 0 by gateOne before this caption reads it, and only
// aXSigT still remembers what it was. On an ordinary running bar the two agree.
gateLbl(float y, bool below, int slot, color col, int openT) =>
    // Captions stay leader-only even though the marks themselves no longer
    // are (washCol, 2026-08-10) - a caption competing with seven others for
    // the same few characters of space is the crowding this section already
    // exists to avoid, and the shape it belongs to still draws, just washed.
    if not na(y) and barstate.isconfirmed and not dimOff(slot)
        sinceTxt = openT > 0 ? ' · від ' + str.format_time(openT, 'HH:mm', 'UTC+1') : ''
        label.new(bar_index - 1, below ? y - atrVal * 0.20 : y + atrVal * 0.20,
             gateTxt(slot) + ' · ' + str.tostring(slotLen(slot)) + sinceTxt, style = label.style_none,
             textcolor = col, size = size.small)

// GENERATED - the caption follows its mark, above or below by the same side.
// The two pointing slots get one call per side, matching their two shapes; only
// one of the pair can be non-na on a bar, and gateLbl draws nothing for the other.
gateLbl(b1Y, eL2, 2, b1Col, b1SigT)
gateLbl(b2Y, eL3, 3, b2Col, b2SigT)
gateLbl(c1Y, eL4, 4, c1Col, c1SigT)
gateLbl(c2Y, eL5, 5, c2Col, c2SigT)
gateLbl(d1Y, eL6, 6, d1Col, d1SigT)
gateLbl(d2Y, eL7, 7, d2Col, d2SigT)

gateLbl(a1YL, true,  0, a1Col, a1SigT)
gateLbl(a1YS, false, 0, a1Col, a1SigT)
gateLbl(a2YL, true,  1, a2Col, a2SigT)
gateLbl(a2YS, false, 1, a2Col, a2SigT)


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW MUCH OF THE CLOSED LOG TO DRAW
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// mar1Scanner.pine's 'Show the log as', brought over, because the same table
// grew the same problem: a full day of closures is taller than the chart and
// there was nothing between all of it and none of it.
//
//   Full list      the summary line and every row under it
//   Summary only   the summary line ALONE - the window, the count, the entries
//                  and the average, which is the number the table exists for
//
// The third state, hidden, is the tick box above: untick 'Show the closed log'.
// A dropdown value for it as well would be two controls for one thing, and two
// controls for one thing is how a table ends up hidden for a reason nobody can
// find.
//
// DECLARED HERE, at the foot, and the block that reads it MOVED DOWN to meet it.
// TradingView maps a saved setting onto an input by POSITION, so a new input
// anywhere above this line slides every setting under it - and the three inputs
// that would have slid are two booleans and an integer, which means saved values
// landing on the wrong switches rather than a clean reset. Moving a drawing block
// costs nothing: tables are independent objects and the order they are filled in
// is not the order they are read.
logDetail = input.string('Full list', 'Show the closed log as', options = ['Full list', 'Summary only'], group = gOut,
     tooltip = "Summary only keeps the header line - the window, the closure count, the entries and the average - and drops every row under it. That one line is what a working screen needs; the rows are for reading back. To hide the table completely, untick 'Show the closed log' above.", display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The closed log
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Newest first, wrapping into blocks beside each other rather than growing down.
// Same five columns as mar1Scanner.pine plus the gate glyph, which is the one
// thing an eight-machine log has to carry and a one-machine log does not.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW BIG THE CLOSED LOG IS DRAWN
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Its own size, separate from every other table, and the reason is that this one
// is read differently from the others. The live tables are glanced at - a row
// appears, you act on it, it goes. The closed log is READ BACK: rows compared
// against each other, times lined up against the chart, an average at the top
// that two settings get judged by. Those are opposite needs, and one setting
// could not serve both - shrinking the log to fit a day of closures shrank the
// live list with it, and enlarging the live list pushed the log off the screen.
//
// 'Same as the tables' is the default and keeps exactly the old behaviour, so
// nothing moves for anyone who does not go looking for this.
//
// DECLARED HERE, at the foot, below every other input in the file. TradingView
// maps a saved setting onto an input by POSITION, so a new input anywhere above
// this line slides every setting under it onto a value saved against something
// else. At the bottom it costs nobody anything. The dialog lays inputs out by
// their GROUP rather than by file order, so it still appears beside the rest of
// the log's settings.
logTxtName = input.string('Same as the tables', 'CLOSED log: text size',
     options = ['Same as the tables', 'Tiny', 'Small', 'Normal', 'Large'], group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'The finished-series log only. Every other table keeps the shared Text size setting above. Use this to fit more rows on screen, or to make a log you are reading back legible without enlarging the live tables with it.', display = display.none)

logTxt = logTxtName == 'Tiny' ? size.tiny : logTxtName == 'Small' ? size.small :
     logTxtName == 'Normal' ? size.normal : logTxtName == 'Large' ? size.large : txtSize

// One tier up, for the Gate column alone - the same relationship txtSizeBig has
// to txtSize, kept intact so the glyph stays the largest thing on a row.
logTxtBig = logTxtName == 'Tiny' ? size.small : logTxtName == 'Small' ? size.normal :
     logTxtName == 'Normal' ? size.large : logTxtName == 'Large' ? size.huge : txtSizeBig

if barstate.islast and showLog
    table.clear(logTable, 0, 0, LOG_BLOCKS * 6 - 2, LOG_TALL + 1)
    rows = array.size(jGate)

    // Read once and named, because four separate places below ask it and four
    // separate spellings of the same test is how one of them ends up different.
    full = logDetail == 'Full list'

    // The summary line, and it is the reason this table is here rather than a
    // list of alerts: closures, entries and the average of the two, which is the
    // same line the scanner prints and the same number maSweepPairs.pine sweeps
    // for. Over ALL rows, not the drawn ones - an average of whatever fitted on
    // screen would move when the text size changed.
    totalEnt = 0

    // AND THE SHAPE THE AVERAGE HIDES, which is the whole reason this histogram
    // sits beside it.
    //
    // One number cannot describe a recovery ladder. Two settings can both average
    // 2.3 entries a win and be nothing alike: one that pays two almost every time
    // is survivable, and one that pays one most of the time and eight
    // occasionally is not. The ladder dies on the TAIL, and the mean is exactly
    // where a tail goes to hide - a handful of very deep windows move it by a
    // tenth and move the account by everything.
    //
    // Seven buckets: how many closures cost one entry, two, three, four, five,
    // and everything past five in one. Six-plus is open-ended on purpose - once a
    // window is that deep the exact number stops changing the decision.
    //
    // ZERO IS A BUCKET TOO, and it is printed only when it is not empty. A window
    // can open on a green diamond and be closed by the very next signal without
    // anything having been entered: it costs nothing, it is a real closure, and it
    // is already pulling the average down. Printing 0x0 on every ordinary day
    // would be noise, so it appears only when there is something to explain.
    //
    // Counted over ALL rows in the window, exactly like the average above it, and
    // not over the rows that happened to fit on screen.
    costHist = array.new_int(7, 0)
    if rows > 0
        for i = 0 to rows - 1
            costE = array.get(jEnt, i)
            totalEnt += costE
            costB = costE < 0 ? 0 : costE > 6 ? 6 : costE
            array.set(costHist, costB, array.get(costHist, costB) + 1)

    // EACH BUCKET CARRIES ITS SHARE AS WELL AS ITS COUNT, because a count on its
    // own is only comparable inside one window. Twelve windows won on the first
    // entry is most of a quiet morning and a fraction of a busy one, so the count
    // answers "how much sample is behind this" and the percentage answers "what
    // is the shape" - and the shape is the half that survives being held against
    // yesterday, or against the same log with one setting moved.
    //
    // Rounded to whole percent, so the buckets can add to 99 or to 101. That is
    // the rounding and not a miscount: the counts beside them are exact and they
    // are what sum to the closure total on the left of the line.
    costTxt = ''
    if rows > 0
        costTxt := '  ·  by cost'
        for cb = 0 to 6
            costN = array.get(costHist, cb)
            // Every bucket from one upwards is printed even when it is empty, so
            // the positions hold still and two logs can be compared by shape
            // rather than by reading the labels each time. Zero is the exception
            // and appears only when it has something in it.
            if cb > 0 or costN > 0
                costTxt := costTxt + '  ' + (cb == 6 ? '6+' : str.tostring(cb)) + '×' + str.tostring(costN) +
                     ' (' + str.tostring(100.0 * costN / rows, '#') + '%)'

    // The block count first, because the automatic row height is derived from
    // it: asking for two blocks and letting the rows size themselves has to
    // spread the closures over two, not over four and then draw half of them.
    maxBlocks = logCols <= 0 ? LOG_BLOCKS : logCols
    autoRows = rows <= 0 ? 20 : int(math.ceil(rows / (1.0 * maxBlocks)))
    autoRows := autoRows < 20 ? 20 : autoRows > LOG_TALL ? LOG_TALL : autoRows
    colRows = logPerCol <= 0 ? autoRows : logPerCol
    shown   = math.min(rows, colRows * maxBlocks)
    blocks  = shown <= 0 ? 1 : math.min(int(math.ceil(shown / math.max(colRows, 1))), maxBlocks)
    hdrSpan = blocks * 6 - 2

    // The window names its own bounds rather than saying "custom", and that is
    // not decoration: it is the one place that proves the setting arrived. Pin
    // 09:00 and if this line still says LOGGED, the script is not reading it.
    //
    // Formatted from the RESULTING timestamps rather than rebuilt from the
    // inputs, which is what makes it a check instead of an echo - it can catch
    // the date picker having landed somewhere other than where you thought,
    // since the picker works in exchange time and this prints UTC+1.
    //
    // periodTo is the first moment PAST the window, so a stretch ending exactly
    // at midnight would be dated for a day it never reaches. Backing off a
    // millisecond asks about the last moment the window actually contains.
    pinFromDay = str.format_time(periodFrom, 'yyyy-MM-dd', 'UTC+1')
    pinEndDay  = str.format_time(periodTo - 1, 'yyyy-MM-dd', 'UTC+1')

    // The second clause is the whole-day case, which the day comparison alone
    // gets wrong: a day starting at midnight ends at the next midnight, whose
    // last contained moment is still the SAME day, so a naive test says no
    // crossing and the label comes out '00:00-00:00' for a window a day wide.
    pinCrosses = spanMs > 0 and (spanMs >= dayMs or pinEndDay != pinFromDay)
    pinToday   = pinFromDay == str.format_time(timenow, 'yyyy-MM-dd', 'UTC+1')
    pinLabel   = str.format_time(periodFrom, pinToday and not pinCrosses ? 'HH:mm' : 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1') +
         (spanMs > 0 ? '-' + str.format_time(periodTo, pinCrosses ? 'MM-dd HH:mm' : 'HH:mm', 'UTC+1') : ' ONWARDS')

    periodName = jrnLength == 'Day' ? 'TODAY' :
         jrnLength == 'Week' ? 'THIS WEEK' :
         jrnLength == 'Everything' and not datePinned ? 'LOGGED' :
         pinned or datePinned ? pinLabel :
         'LAST ' + str.tostring(lengthHours, '#.##') + 'H'

    // The date is read ONLY when the anchor says to read it, so under the other
    // two anchors the picker is inert - and inert silently, which is the whole
    // problem: nothing on screen distinguished a date that was applied from one
    // that was ignored. Compared against the default rather than shown whenever
    // the anchor is wrong, or it would be permanent for everyone who has never
    // opened the picker.
    dateIgnored = not datePinned and jrnFrom != JRN_FROM_DEFAULT

    hdBg = dateIgnored ? #553300 : #12261A
    table.cell(logTable, 0, 0,
         ' CLOSED ' + periodName + '  ·  ' + str.tostring(rows) + ' closed  ·  ' + str.tostring(totalEnt) + ' entries, ' +
         (rows > 0 ? str.tostring(1.0 * totalEnt / rows, '#.00000') : '-') + ' avg' +
         costTxt +
         // WHICH MACHINES THIS AVERAGE IS OF. It is the number maSweepPairs.pine
         // sweeps and the one two settings are compared by, so an average over
         // six slots must never be mistaken for an average over eight. An
         // unticked gate now really is absent from the log rather than silently
         // included, which is what makes saying so necessary.
         (liveGates < 8 ? '  ·  ' + str.tostring(liveGates) + ' of 8 slots' : '') +
         (shown < rows ? '  ·  newest ' + str.tostring(shown) + ' listed' : '') +
         // The shape, whenever either half of it was typed rather than worked
         // out. A layout setting that leaves no trace on screen is one you
         // cannot prove arrived.
         (logPerCol > 0 or logCols > 0 ? '  ·  ' + str.tostring(blocks) + ' x ' + str.tostring(colRows) + ' by hand' : '') +
         (rows >= JRN_MAX ? '  ·  AT THE ' + str.tostring(JRN_MAX) + ' ROW CEILING' : '') +
         (dateIgnored ? "  ·  DATE IGNORED - set 'Window anchored' to Starting at date" : '') + ' ',
         bgcolor = hdBg, text_color = dateIgnored ? color.orange : shown < rows or rows >= JRN_MAX ? #FFC163 : #6EF0A5, text_size = logTxt,
         tooltip = 'by cost is the distribution behind the average: 1× is how many windows were won on the first entry, 2× on the second, and so on, with 6+ collecting everything deeper, each followed by its share of the whole window in brackets. The average to its left is the same closures in one number and it cannot show a tail - two settings with an identical average can have completely different shapes here, and it is the tail a recovery ladder dies on. The shares are rounded to whole percent and can sum to 99 or 101; the counts beside them are exact. A 0× bucket appears only when a window closed without anything having been entered.')
    // The filler cells and the merge only when there are rows under them. In
    // Summary only the header is a single line, and stretching it across the
    // columns of a table with nothing in them would leave it reaching over air.
    if full
        for k = 1 to hdrSpan
            table.cell(logTable, k, 0, '', bgcolor = hdBg, text_size = logTxt)
        table.merge_cells(logTable, 0, 0, hdrSpan, 0)

    // Everything below the summary line belongs to Full list. That one line is
    // the whole of what a working screen needs; the rows are for reading back.
    if full
        for b = 0 to blocks - 1
            h = b * 6
            table.cell(logTable, h + 0, 1, ' Gate ',    bgcolor = color.gray, text_color = color.white, text_size = logTxt)
            table.cell(logTable, h + 1, 1, ' Opened ',  bgcolor = color.gray, text_color = color.white, text_size = logTxt,
                 tooltip = 'When the FIRST ENTRY of that window was taken - not when the gate opened, which can be a quarter of an hour earlier and had cost nothing yet. UTC+1. The gate opening is what decides which machine keeps the row when windows overlap, but it is not printed: a closed series is dated from the first thing that cost. The RUNNING table dates its rows from the gate instead, because it answers a different question: since when has this slot been demanding attention. maRejection.pine stamps the same first-entry time on its success label.')
            table.cell(logTable, h + 2, 1, ' Closed ',  bgcolor = color.gray, text_color = color.white, text_size = logTxt,
                 tooltip = 'Closing time of the winning candle, UTC+1.')
            table.cell(logTable, h + 3, 1, ' Entries ', bgcolor = color.gray, text_color = color.white, text_size = logTxt,
                 tooltip = 'How many entries that window cost, the winning one included. Coloured by the rung it closed on - the same ladder the diamonds wear.')
            table.cell(logTable, h + 4, 1, ' Side ',    bgcolor = color.gray, text_color = color.white, text_size = logTxt)

    // i is the drawn position, k the row in the arrays. They run in opposite
    // directions: the log is stored oldest first and read newest first.
    if full
        for i = 0 to shown - 1
            k = rows - 1 - i
            g = array.get(jGate, k)
            en = array.get(jEnt, k)
            sd = array.get(jDir, k)
            rung = array.get(jStep, k)
            blk = int(math.floor(i / math.max(colRows, 1)))
            c = blk * 6
            rr = i - blk * colRows + 2
            // THE ROW IS THE RUNG IT CLOSED ON, which is what makes this log and the
            // live table one story rather than two. A gate sits above as a purple
            // row, closes, and reappears here as the same purple row - nothing
            // changes colour on the way across, so a finished series can be
            // recognised as the one that was being watched.
            //
            // Deeper transparency than the live table on purpose - 62 against 55.
            // This is history and must not compete with a window still open.
            rowBg = rungFill(rung, 62)
            rowFg = rungText(rung)
            table.cell(logTable, c + 0, rr, ' ' + slotGlyph(g) + ' ' + str.tostring(slotLen(g)) + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = logTxtBig)
            table.cell(logTable, c + 1, rr, ' ' + str.format_time(array.get(jOpen, k), 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1') + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = logTxt)
            table.cell(logTable, c + 2, rr, ' ' + str.format_time(array.get(jClose, k), 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1') + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = logTxt)
            table.cell(logTable, c + 3, rr, ' ' + str.tostring(en) + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = logTxt)
            // Same hue, darkened on a light rung and paled on the top one: lime on
            // lime and red on red are both invisible, and on the red step the row
            // already carries the alarm. Only rung 1 needs it now - see the same
            // note above rungFill.
            logLight = rung == 1
            logHot   = rung == 6 or rung == 8
            table.cell(logTable, c + 4, rr, sd == 1 ? ' ▲ ' : ' ▼ ', bgcolor = rowBg,
                 text_color = sd == 1 ? (logLight ? color.new(#006622, 0) : logHot ? #B9F6CA : color.lime) :
                      (logLight ? color.new(#991111, 0) : logHot ? #FFFFFF : color.red), text_size = logTxt)

    if rows == 0 and full
        table.cell(logTable, 0, 2, ' nothing closed yet ', bgcolor = color.new(#333333, 60), text_color = color.silver, text_size = logTxt)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Live preview - the marker on the candle that is still forming
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// maRejection.pine's own live/repainting preview, brought over. The header
// used to say this file drew no intrabar previews, on the grounds that eight
// machines drawing on every tick was eight markers moving at once - a fair
// objection when all eight machines drew. It stopped being true the moment
// only the leader ever draws: there is at most one marker to preview now, the
// same one dimOff already lets through, so the crowding this was rejected for
// no longer happens on an ordinary bar.
//
// WHY IT LIVES HERE, at the foot of the file, and not beside the signal it
// projects. livePreview and liveFade are inputs, and TradingView maps a saved
// setting onto an input by POSITION - every input above this line already has
// a position on every chart carrying this script, so a new one has to go
// after all of them or it slides every setting under it onto the wrong value.
// gateOne() cannot read livePreview directly for the same reason in reverse:
// it is CALLED hundreds of lines above this point, and an input declared here
// does not exist yet when that call runs. So gateOne() hands out seven raw
// ingredients instead - triggerLong, triggerShort, buyFound, sellFound,
// lastLongBar, lastShortBar, carriedAtSignal - and the live verdict, cooldown
// projection included, is built from them down here, after livePreview is
// finally in scope.
//
// PREVIEW MARKERS REPAINT, exactly as maRejection.pine's do and for the same
// reason: they are read from the candle currently forming, whose high, low
// and close are all still moving. A preview can appear, change colour, or
// vanish within one candle - only the solid marker that lands after the close
// is a fact. That is the whole trade: an earlier, less certain look at the
// same signal, in exchange for a marker that might not survive to the close.
gLive = 'Live preview'

livePreview = input.bool(true, 'Show a fading marker on the forming candle', group = gLive,
     tooltip = 'A repainting preview of the leading gate - the same one dimOff already lets through - read from the candle still forming. It can appear, change colour or vanish before that candle closes; only the solid marker afterwards is settled. Off leaves the chart exactly as it was before this existed.', display = display.none)
liveFade = input.int(45, 'Preview transparency', minval = 0, maxval = 90, group = gLive, display = display.none)

// Only the candle currently being built, and only on a real-time connection -
// a replay or a historical bar has no "still forming" state to preview.
liveBar = livePreview and barstate.isrealtime and not barstate.isconfirmed

// One projection, shared by all eight slots. Mirrors maRejection.pine's own
// liveStreak / liveGateOk / liveGateArmed, with the same deliberate
// simplification that file makes: gateThreshold (thr) is NOT projected,
// because in Distribution and Max of both it is read back from the streak
// histogram, and an honest projection would have to simulate that histogram
// update too. The threshold can move by at most one bucket at the close, so a
// preview can occasionally offer a marker the settled pass then withholds -
// the same known limit maRejection.pine accepts, and a smaller lie than the
// repainting already is.
//
// trL / trS are triggerLong / triggerShort read WITHOUT the [1] offset the
// settled buyFound / sellFound use - the entire trick.
//
// THE COOLDOWN IS PROJECTED ONE BAR FORWARD, and it did not used to be - this
// function used to take canFireLong / canFireShort straight from gateOne and
// treat them as already correct, on the reasoning that they only reference
// lastLongBar / lastShortBar and those only change once a bar CONFIRMS. That
// reasoning missed the one case it exists to cover: bf / sf below (buyFound /
// sellFound, the SETTLED signal on THIS same candle, using triggerLong[1])
// have not been graded yet either, but they are about to be - at this
// candle's own close, gateOne will stamp lastLongBar / lastShortBar to
// bar_index the moment buyFound or sellFound goes through. A preview built
// from the OLD lastLongBar therefore told a bar was free to fire when the
// bar's own settled signal was about to spend that exact budget one line
// later - a preview offering an entry the settled pass would never have
// allowed. liveLastLong / liveLastShort below are maRejection.pine's own fix
// for this, unchanged: project the spend forward before asking whether the
// cooldown allows it.
livePrev(bool trL, bool trS, bool bf, bool sf, int llb, int lsb, int streak, int thr, int lim, color carC) =>
    liveLastLong  = bf or (not perDirectionCooldown and sf) ? bar_index : llb
    liveLastShort = sf or (not perDirectionCooldown and bf) ? bar_index : lsb
    liveCanLong   = bar_index + 1 - liveLastLong >= cooldown
    liveCanShort  = bar_index + 1 - liveLastShort >= cooldown
    // flatOkLive, not flatOk - the LIVE, tick-by-tick reading, not the one
    // frozen until this candle confirms. See the note beside flatOkLive's
    // definition in MODULE: flat filter: the confirmed flatOk is a whole
    // candle stale during formation by construction, and a star gated on it
    // could hold through a candle's own close while the real-time ratio had
    // already crossed into a flat - the same class of fault the cooldown
    // projection above exists to avoid, just one bar further out.
    lFoundL = trL and liveCanLong and hoursOk and flatOkLive
    lFoundS = trS and not trL and liveCanShort and hoursOk and flatOkLive
    lStreak = (lFoundL and not isBull) or (lFoundS and not isBear) ? streak + 1 : (lFoundL or lFoundS) ? 0 : streak
    lGateOk    = lStreak >= thr
    lGateArmed = not gateIsOff and lStreak == thr - 1
    lBuy   = liveBar and lFoundL and lGateOk
    lSell  = liveBar and lFoundS and lGateOk
    lBuyW  = liveBar and lFoundL and lGateArmed
    lSellW = liveBar and lFoundS and lGateArmed
    lMissed  = (lBuy or lBuyW) and not isBull or (lSell or lSellW) and not isBear
    lCarried = (lBuy or lSell or lBuyW or lSellW) ? (lMissed ? streakColor(lStreak, lim) : na) : carC
    lCol     = na(lCarried) ? streakColor(lStreak, lim) : lCarried
    [lBuy, lSell, lBuyW, lSellW, lCol]

[a1LBuy, a1LSell, a1LBuyW, a1LSellW, a1LCol] = livePrev(a1TrL, a1TrS, a1Bf, a1Sf, a1LLB, a1LSB, a1Streak, a1Thr, a1Lim, a1CarC)
[a2LBuy, a2LSell, a2LBuyW, a2LSellW, a2LCol] = livePrev(a2TrL, a2TrS, a2Bf, a2Sf, a2LLB, a2LSB, a2Streak, a2Thr, a2Lim, a2CarC)
[b1LBuy, b1LSell, b1LBuyW, b1LSellW, b1LCol] = livePrev(b1TrL, b1TrS, b1Bf, b1Sf, b1LLB, b1LSB, b1Streak, b1Thr, b1Lim, b1CarC)
[b2LBuy, b2LSell, b2LBuyW, b2LSellW, b2LCol] = livePrev(b2TrL, b2TrS, b2Bf, b2Sf, b2LLB, b2LSB, b2Streak, b2Thr, b2Lim, b2CarC)
[c1LBuy, c1LSell, c1LBuyW, c1LSellW, c1LCol] = livePrev(c1TrL, c1TrS, c1Bf, c1Sf, c1LLB, c1LSB, c1Streak, c1Thr, c1Lim, c1CarC)
[c2LBuy, c2LSell, c2LBuyW, c2LSellW, c2LCol] = livePrev(c2TrL, c2TrS, c2Bf, c2Sf, c2LLB, c2LSB, c2Streak, c2Thr, c2Lim, c2CarC)
[d1LBuy, d1LSell, d1LBuyW, d1LSellW, d1LCol] = livePrev(d1TrL, d1TrS, d1Bf, d1Sf, d1LLB, d1LSB, d1Streak, d1Thr, d1Lim, d1CarC)
[d2LBuy, d2LSell, d2LBuyW, d2LSellW, d2LCol] = livePrev(d2TrL, d2TrS, d2Bf, d2Sf, d2LLB, d2LSB, d2Streak, d2Thr, d2Lim, d2CarC)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE BIG MARKET INDICATOR - overwrites the Market cell after the fact
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Declared here, after livePrev, for the same reason livePrev itself sits at
// the foot of the file: it needs the live-preview results (a1LBuy and the
// rest), and those do not exist yet at the point the RUNNING table itself is
// drawn, further up. Rather than move that table's own hundred and fifty
// lines down here - which would leave them orphaned from the header, legend
// and footer they belong with - this repaints one cell: the Market column,
// on whichever row runRow recorded for this slot.
//
// It fires on the same two facts the small text in that cell already reports
// - inMkt (a settled entry, live on the forming candle) and the live-preview
// flags (an entry or a warning the forming candle is CURRENTLY offering,
// repainting exactly as the chart's own fading marker does - see livePrev
// above) - just drawn large enough and bright enough that a glance across the
// table finds it, instead of a word that has to be read.
//
// IT DISAPPEARS ON ITS OWN, without a timer. inMkt is a settled entry's own
// buy/sell flag, true for exactly the candle it fired on and not the next;
// the preview flags are gated on barstate.isrealtime and not
// barstate.isconfirmed, so they are true only while THIS candle is still
// forming and are false again the instant it closes.
if barstate.islast and showTable
    for s = 0 to 7
        row = array.get(runRow, s)
        if row >= 0
            inMktBig = s == 0 ? a1Buy or a1Sell : s == 1 ? a2Buy or a2Sell : s == 2 ? b1Buy or b1Sell : s == 3 ? b2Buy or b2Sell :
                 s == 4 ? c1Buy or c1Sell : s == 5 ? c2Buy or c2Sell : s == 6 ? d1Buy or d1Sell : d2Buy or d2Sell
            lBuy   = s == 0 ? a1LBuy   : s == 1 ? a2LBuy   : s == 2 ? b1LBuy   : s == 3 ? b2LBuy   :
                 s == 4 ? c1LBuy   : s == 5 ? c2LBuy   : s == 6 ? d1LBuy   : d2LBuy
            lSell  = s == 0 ? a1LSell  : s == 1 ? a2LSell  : s == 2 ? b1LSell  : s == 3 ? b2LSell  :
                 s == 4 ? c1LSell  : s == 5 ? c2LSell  : s == 6 ? d1LSell  : d2LSell
            lBuyW  = s == 0 ? a1LBuyW  : s == 1 ? a2LBuyW  : s == 2 ? b1LBuyW  : s == 3 ? b2LBuyW  :
                 s == 4 ? c1LBuyW  : s == 5 ? c2LBuyW  : s == 6 ? d1LBuyW  : d2LBuyW
            lSellW = s == 0 ? a1LSellW : s == 1 ? a2LSellW : s == 2 ? b1LSellW : s == 3 ? b2LSellW :
                 s == 4 ? c1LSellW : s == 5 ? c2LSellW : s == 6 ? d1LSellW : d2LSellW

            bigTxt = inMktBig ? ' ● IN ' :
                 lBuy   ? ' ~ entry ▲ ' :
                 lSell  ? ' ~ entry ▼ ' :
                 lBuyW  ? ' ~ warn ▲ ' :
                 lSellW ? ' ~ warn ▼ ' : ''
            if bigTxt != ''
                bigBg = inMktBig ? color.new(#00E676, 0) : (lBuy or lSell) ? color.new(#CCFF00, 0) : color.new(#FFC107, 0)
                // Capped at txtSizeBig, not size.huge - with up to 8 rows,
                // huge on every one of them at once pushed the table taller
                // than the chart (user report, 2026-08-10).
                table.cell(runTable, 2, row, bigTxt, bgcolor = bigBg, text_color = color.black, text_size = txtSizeBig)

// WHY A LABEL AND NOT A NINTH FAMILY OF plotshape / plotchar CALLS. The plot
// budget is spoken for: the eight settled entries and eight outcomes already
// spend 40 of the 46 plots the seventeen alertconditions and the background
// leave, six spare. Eight more shape families at two plots each is sixteen -
// RE10140 again, the same ceiling this file has hit twice already.
//
// A label costs no plot at all - the same fact that moved the entry captions
// off plotshape's const text and onto gateLbl. So the preview is a label too,
// its text the SAME glyph slotGlyph(slot) already gives the real mark, sized
// up to read as the big, hard-to-miss shape this was asked for, coloured with
// the same fade every preview in maRejection.pine wears.
//
// EIGHT HANDLES, one per slot, because a label does not update itself the way
// a plot does - every tick that still qualifies would otherwise stack a new
// label on the last one instead of replacing it. Deleting the previous label
// before drawing the next is what turns that pile into a single flickering
// glyph, which is the whole point: an entry that is only briefly true should
// look like it is appearing and disappearing, not like it never happened.
var label a1LLbl = na
var label a2LLbl = na
var label b1LLbl = na
var label b2LLbl = na
var label c1LLbl = na
var label c2LLbl = na
var label d1LLbl = na
var label d2LLbl = na

// Same dense-lane placement the settled marks use, worked out from the eight
// live flags rather than the settled ones - almost always at most one is
// true at once, since dimOff already limits the settled marks to the leader,
// but the rare bar where NOBODY is leading yet can still see more than one
// slot's live trigger firing together, exactly as the one-tick-lag note above
// describes for the settled marks. The lane keeps that bar from stacking them.
lF0 = a1LBuy or a1LSell or a1LBuyW or a1LSellW
lF1 = a2LBuy or a2LSell or a2LBuyW or a2LSellW
lF2 = b1LBuy or b1LSell or b1LBuyW or b1LSellW
lF3 = b2LBuy or b2LSell or b2LBuyW or b2LSellW
lF4 = c1LBuy or c1LSell or c1LBuyW or c1LSellW
lF5 = c2LBuy or c2LSell or c2LBuyW or c2LSellW
lF6 = d1LBuy or d1LSell or d1LBuyW or d1LSellW
lF7 = d2LBuy or d2LSell or d2LBuyW or d2LSellW

// No offset and no [1]: the preview sits on THIS bar, the one it is read
// from, unlike the settled marks which land one bar back on the candle that
// already closed.
liveY(bool lng, bool sht, int ln) =>
    gap = atrVal * (0.35 + ln * 0.50)
    lng ? low - gap : sht ? high + gap : na

// A solid star for the whole preview, long or short, rather than the eight
// per-slot glyphs (user request, 2026-08-10) - one authentic, unmistakable
// symbol that reads as "preview" on sight whichever of the eight machines
// fired, instead of eight different shapes that first have to be decoded
// through slotGlyph. Long gets the filled star, short its outline
// counterpart - the same solid/hollow pairing Unicode already uses for
// black and white star, so the two read as one glyph in two states rather
// than two unrelated ones.
//
liveMark(bool on, label handle, int slot, bool lng, bool sht, color col, int ln) =>
    if not na(handle)
        label.delete(handle)
    label lbl = na
    if on and not dimOff(slot) and (lng or sht)
        y = liveY(lng, sht, ln)
        if not na(y)
            lbl := label.new(bar_index, y, lng ? '★' : '☆', style = label.style_none,
                 textcolor = color.new(col, liveFade), size = size.large)
    lbl

a1LLbl := liveMark(liveA, a1LLbl, 0, a1LBuy or a1LBuyW, a1LSell or a1LSellW, a1LCol, lane(0, lF0, lF1, lF2, lF3, lF4, lF5, lF6, lF7))
a2LLbl := liveMark(liveA, a2LLbl, 1, a2LBuy or a2LBuyW, a2LSell or a2LSellW, a2LCol, lane(1, lF0, lF1, lF2, lF3, lF4, lF5, lF6, lF7))
b1LLbl := liveMark(liveB, b1LLbl, 2, b1LBuy or b1LBuyW, b1LSell or b1LSellW, b1LCol, lane(2, lF0, lF1, lF2, lF3, lF4, lF5, lF6, lF7))
b2LLbl := liveMark(liveB, b2LLbl, 3, b2LBuy or b2LBuyW, b2LSell or b2LSellW, b2LCol, lane(3, lF0, lF1, lF2, lF3, lF4, lF5, lF6, lF7))
c1LLbl := liveMark(liveC, c1LLbl, 4, c1LBuy or c1LBuyW, c1LSell or c1LSellW, c1LCol, lane(4, lF0, lF1, lF2, lF3, lF4, lF5, lF6, lF7))
c2LLbl := liveMark(liveC, c2LLbl, 5, c2LBuy or c2LBuyW, c2LSell or c2LSellW, c2LCol, lane(5, lF0, lF1, lF2, lF3, lF4, lF5, lF6, lF7))
d1LLbl := liveMark(liveD, d1LLbl, 6, d1LBuy or d1LBuyW, d1LSell or d1LSellW, d1LCol, lane(6, lF0, lF1, lF2, lF3, lF4, lF5, lF6, lF7))
d2LLbl := liveMark(liveD, d2LLbl, 7, d2LBuy or d2LBuyW, d2LSell or d2LSellW, d2LCol, lane(7, lF0, lF1, lF2, lF3, lF4, lF5, lF6, lF7))

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FLAT, shaded - optional, and moved here
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Two shades, unchanged from when this lived beside flatOn: the darker wash
// is the flat itself, the pale tail behind it is the flatWait bars the
// filter keeps standing aside for after the ratio has recovered. All eight
// machines are drawn on these candles, so a shaded stretch with no markers
// in it is the filter working, and a shaded stretch your own eye would call
// a trend is flatLen or the two thresholds being wrong.
//
// DECLARED HERE, at the very foot of the file, and not beside flatOn where
// it used to sit. TradingView maps a saved setting onto an input by
// POSITION, and a new input inserted back there would have slid every input
// after it - showHours, showSecond, the closed-log settings, livePreview and
// the rest - onto the wrong saved value on any chart already carrying this
// script. bgcolor() does not care where in the file it is called from, only
// what its own condition evaluates to on this bar, so moving the CALL costs
// nothing; only a new INPUT declared mid-file would have.
//
// OFF turns off the paint alone. flatOn still suppresses signals and the
// streak still holds still while the filter is active either way - this
// input decides nothing but whether that state gets drawn, for anyone who
// wants the filter without the chart repainting a background on every tick.
showFlatBg = input.bool(true, 'Shade flat stretches on the chart', group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'The grey wash over stretches the flat filter is standing aside for. Unrelated to whether it suppresses signals - Stand aside while flat above still controls that either way. Untick to stop the background repainting and keep the filter itself exactly as it was.', display = display.none)

// flatNowLive / flatHoldLive, not the confirmed flatNow / flatHold - the wash
// now follows the ratio tick by tick on the forming candle instead of
// holding the last confirmed shade until the close (user request, 2026-08-11:
// the star preview had the same staleness and was fixed the same way - see
// flatOkLive in MODULE: flat filter). What is drawn on every CLOSED candle is
// unchanged either way, since flatNowLive and flatNow agree the instant a
// candle confirms; only the forming candle's own shade can now arrive early.
bgcolor(not showFlatBg or not flatOn ? na : flatNowLive ? color.new(#6E7B8B, 86) : flatHoldLive ? color.new(#6E7B8B, 94) : na,
     title = 'Flat')
